\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar sheds gains, looks to U.S. retail data. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hideyuki Sano \par \uc2 794 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 November 2001\par 08:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Nov 14 (Reuters) - The dollar shed some of its offshore gains on Wednesday as optimism that the U.S.-led military action in Afghanistan could come to a quick end started to fade. \par \par \uc2  News that the U.S.-supported Northern Alliance had captured the Afghan capital of Kabul from the ruling Taliban on Tuesday spurred the greenback to three-month highs against the euro and pushed it through resistance of 121.30 against the yen. \par \par \uc2  In Tokyo trade, the dollar climbed to a fresh one-week high against the yen at 121.80 yen, but later slipped below late New York levels. \par \par \uc2  Traders noted that the most difficult part of the U.S.-led "war against terrorism" had yet to come. \par \par \uc2  "The fall of Kabul does not necessarily mean the U.S. has won the war," a trader at a Japanese trust bank said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts agreed the United States was still far from achieving its objectives in Afghanistan. \par \par \uc2  "The hard work of finding terrorists and ferreting them out from caves still remains," said Ken Landon, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank in a research note. "Investors should consequently proceed with caution toward both the dollar and equities." \par \par \uc2  Nobuya Shida, vice president at JP Morgan Chase, said: "I don't think there's any reason the dollar should go up beyond where it was before the September 11 attacks, even if it is bought because of brighter prospects for the U.S. military campaign in Afghanistan." \par \par \uc2  The dollar traded at around 122 yen shortly before the hijack attacks on the World Trade Center and the Pentagon. \par \par \uc2  The dollar got a boost from remarks by a Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official and speculation that Furukawa Electric Co Ltd was buying dollars to help fund its purchase of the fibre-optic cable division of Lucent Technology . \par \par \uc2  Senior Japanese Finance Ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi said on Wednesday: "...It is not favourable if the yen moves in a speculative manner. Our stance remains unchanged, that we would quickly prevent such a move." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said Mizoguchi's comments, not made for the first time, would have little effect on dollar-yen exchange rates at current levels but were effective in reminding the market that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop the dollar's fall. \par \par \uc2  RANGE STILL HOLDS \par \par \uc2  The greenback was bid around 121.55 as of 0630 GMT, slightly down from its late New York level of 121.64. \par \par \uc2  The euro maintained an even keel and was bid at 88.17 cents to the dollar at 0630 GMT, compared with a three-month low of 87.80 offshore. \par \par \uc2  The break of support around 88.20/50 was seen as bearish, and dealers were eyeing August 8 lows around 87.35/40. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  struggled to hold above the 107 mark. It was bid around 107.18 in late morning. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the dollar-yen rate would likely stay in a tight range for the time being, supported by MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  threats on the downside but lacking the energy to break through a high around 123.40, reached on October 25. \par \par \uc2  "Trade volume is getting thin these days because of the global slowdown," Shida of JP Morgan Chase said. "So I expect the dollar to trade in a narrow range." \par \par \uc2  Looking ahead, the market was focusing on U.S. October retail sales data, due at 1330 GMT. The average forecast among economists polled by Reuters was for a rise of 2.7 percent after the September 11 attacks on the United States led to a 2.4 percent fall in September. \par \par \uc2  "The market is expecting firm retail numbers," said Takashi Toyahara, manager at Nomura Securities said. "But the key will be Christmas sales. So the dollar is unlikely to gain much at the moment." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0651 GMT. All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 \par \par \uc2  GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                        Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2000 Cls\par                                 13 Nov.\par                        -----------------------------------------\par  Euro/dlr              0.8820   0.8807   +0.15    -7.12   0.9496\par  Dlr/yen               121.50   121.64   -0.12    +6.16   114.45\par  Euro/yen              107.15   107.12   +0.03    -1.44   108.71\par  Dlr/swiss             1.6676   1.6703   -0.16    +4.15   1.6012\par  Stg/dlr               1.4428   1.4411   +0.12    -3.81   1.5000\par  Can/dlr               1.5935   1.5937   -0.01    +6.85   1.4914\par  Aus/dlr               0.5214   0.5205   +0.17    -6.79   0.5594\par  Euro/swiss            1.4708   1.4709   -0.01    -3.37   1.5221\par  Euro/stg              0.6109   0.6108   +0.02    -3.42   0.6325.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011114dxbe00dwd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
