\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dlr Rises Against Yen On  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Fears \b0\par\par\uc2  By Miyako Takebe \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 818 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 2001\par 06:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The dollar advanced against the yen in Asia Tuesday as a reminder \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  stands ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trade and the receding likelihood of terrorist ties to the New York plane crash prompted players to buy back the U.S unit. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese Finance Ministry was quick to warn against yen strength after the dollar dipped below Y120 overnight following reports American Airlines Flight 587 had crashed just after takeoff from Kennedy International Airport. \par \par \uc2  "We maintain the view that excessive yen strengthening is undesirable," Zembei Mizoguchi, International Bureau Chief at the MOF, said. \par \par \uc2  "We are watching markets carefully, and will take action if needed." \par \par \uc2  The comments helped give the greenback an extra boost after it found support in early Asian trading from weak Tokyo share prices and short-covering linked to the diminishing possibility the plane crash was terrorist related, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "The weak Nikkei (225 Stock Average), Mizoguchi's comments and the weak euro were behind the dollar's gradual rise today," said Koichi Udagawa, trader at ABN Amro Bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  With its rebound from its overnight lows, the dollar "may have temporarily hit bottom against the yen," Udagawa said, pointing out that the market had been looking at the possibility of a dollar fall to Y119 or Y118 before the plane crash. \par \par \uc2  But the greenback's rise Tuesday remained contained below Y121.00 amid a lack of reasons to actively chase the dollar higher, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "Although the plane crash may not be linked to terrorists, its effect on already weak sentiment (toward the U.S.) isn't very good," said a senior Japan bank trader. \par \par \uc2  "People are looking for \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell - which at the moment seems to be the euro," Udagawa added. \par \par \uc2  At 0515 GMT (12:15 a.m. EST), the dollar was quoted at Y120.91, above Y120.40 late Monday in New York. The dollar was at Y120.46 late Monday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $0.8914, lower than $0.8945 late Monday in New York. The euro was at $0.8947 in Tokyo Monday. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y107.78, up from Y107.70 in New York late Monday. The euro was at Y107.66 in Tokyo late Monday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar, which later broke above Y121.00 for the first time since Thursday, got an additional boost from reports the Taliban have deserted Kabul, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "(The news) happened in Tokyo hours, so I bet the dollar/yen will get bought again when London players come in," said a trader at a U.S. investment house trader. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also continued to strengthen against the euro amid lingering concerns over the euro-zone economies and the sliding credibility of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite its surprise 50 basis point cut last week, ABN's Udagawa said. \par \par \uc2  But selling of the euro/yen, which had been the main driver behind the recent decline in the dollar/yen, may temporarily be coming to a halt, said the senior Japanese bank trader. \par \par \uc2  Outstanding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures positions as of Friday, published Monday by the International Monetary Market in Chicago showed the market has shed a lot of its long euro positions, he said. \par \par \uc2  "Even if position adjustments start pushing down the euro/yen and dollar/yen again, the effects are likely to be limited at this stage" given the remaining term and value of positions, the trader said. \par \par \uc2  In the coming weeks, the dollar is likely to stay between Y120.00 and Y125.00, as fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities will support the downside, while the possibility that the government may backpedal on structural reforms would push up the greenback up against the yen, said Toru Umemoto, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Morgan Stanley in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "With the dollar/yen at the bottom of the range, the risks would be on the upside," Umemoto said. \par \par \uc2  U.S. credit ratings agency Standard and Poor's said Tuesday that default risks at Japanese companies are increasing amid the slow pace of structural reform. \par \par \uc2  "The slow pace of structural reforms has increased credit risk. It's better to take your medicine sooner than later," said Rob Richards, managing director of corporate and government ratings at S&P. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Sterling was at $1.4520, down from $1.4545 in New York and lower than\par $1.4572 in Tokyo Monday.\par   -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par miyako.takebe@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The chart of late Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates which accompanied Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has\par been replaced with an enhanced \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fixture which is updated\par hourly.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Subscribers can find this table:\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -On Telerate pages 4046 and 1087.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -On Dow Jones Newswires by searching the code N/IFX\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \