\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar surges as Taliban grip weakens. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Robin Shepherd \par \uc2 556 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 2001\par 12:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Nov 13 (Reuters) - The dollar hit two month highs against the euro on Tuesday on news that anti-Taliban fighters had entered the Afghan capital, extending early gains built on reports that Monday's U.S. air crash was probably an accident. \par \par \uc2  Fears that the New York plane crash was a follow-up to the September 11 attacks had prompted safe haven buying, with the euro, yen and Swiss franc all gaining at the dollar's expense. \par \par \uc2  But indications that the crash was an accident sparked a sharp reversal and reports that the Taliban's grip on power was slipping away in Afghanistan further boosted dollar-positive sentiment. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is strengthening today because the Taliban is on the run," said Jesper Dannesboe, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein in London. "This is the key driver behind \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets." \par \par \uc2  The dollar's gains were spectacular. It rose almost one percent against the euro to hit an intra day high of $0.8856. Against the Swiss franc it was up by more than one percent at 1.6369, a two week high. And against the yen it was up almost three quarters of a percent at 121.28. \par \par \uc2  The pound also took heart from dollar strength, hitting a 10 week high against the euro beyond 61.13 pence. \par \par \uc2  AFGHAN VICTORIES BUOY DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  U.S.-backed anti-Taliban fighters entered the Afghan capital Kabul on Tuesday as reports from across the country pointed to a collapse of Taliban rule. \par \par \uc2  Since fears over the efficacy of the U.S. bombing campaign had previously weighed upon the dollar, dealers said the latest reports were bound to be a force for upward momentum in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "I suppose it is about political risk. It wasn't so long ago some people were talking about the U.S. being in this one for years. The dollar is definitely going to be helped by these victories," said one trader. \par \par \uc2  Many of Afghanistan's Taliban rulers appeared to have slipped away under cover of night, abandoning a city they had held since winning a civil war in the mid-1990s. \par \par \uc2  On the macro-economic front, the dollar's rise against the euro was helped by disappointing French inflation data. French consumer prices unexpectedly rose in October, denting the prospect of further growth-boosting interest rate cuts from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Traders said German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder's threat to resign and break up the ruling coalition if it did not back him in a vote on troop mobilisation for Afghanistan was also weighing on euro sentiment. \par \par \uc2  JAPAN KNOCKS YEN \par \par \uc2  The dollar's rally against the yen was further aided when Japan's senior finance ministry official, Zembei Mizoguchi, reiterated that the government stood ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap yen strength. \par \par \uc2  Talk that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Furukawa Electric Co Ltd was buying dollars to complete its purchase of a U.S. optical unit from Lucent Technologies Inc also swirled through trading floors. \par \par \uc2  The original $2.75 billion deal has been delayed twice and Furukawa faces a deadline of November 16 to settle. \par \par \uc2  Indications of a strong opening on Wall Street were also helping the dollar. The Nasdaq December future was up by more than a percent at 1,544.5. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011113dxbd00lrh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
