\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar recovers from shock of airline crash. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hideyuki Sano \par \uc2 688 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 2001\par 05:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Nov 13 (Reuters) - The dollar recovered on Tuesday from the initial shock of an airliner crash in New York after U.S. authorities said the tragedy was likely an accident rather than a follow-up to the September 11 hijack attacks. \par \par \uc2  Dealers dumped the greenback immediately after the American Airline Airbus A-300 carrying 251 passengers and nine crew crashed in Queens, New York, shortly after takeoff from John F. Kennedy airport on Monday. \par \par \uc2  But the greenback rebounded after U.S. officials said evidence so far suggested it was a devastating accident. \par \par \uc2  "Everybody thought it was a terrorist attack and sold the dollar," said Jun Kato, a manager at Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "But for now that doesn't seem to be the case, so people are buying it back." \par \par \uc2  The dollar regained its pre-crash levels against most \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It rose to 120.88/93 yen from 120.40 yen in late New York trade and more than a full yen from a fresh one-month low of 119.69 yen immediately after the crash. \par \par \uc2  It also rose against the euro, which slumped to 89.16/21 cents from 89.42 cents in late U.S. trade and from a Monday high of 90.17 cents. The euro was little changed on the yen at 107.76/78 yen . \par \par \uc2  The dollar also recovered its pre-crash levels against Swiss franc, a traditional safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The greenback traded at 1.6440 franc , up from 1.6365 in late U.S. trade and sharply higher than lows near 1.62. \par \par \uc2  MOF FLEXES MUSCLES \par \par \uc2  The dollar also got a boost from remarks by a Japanese official that the current economic situation warranted a stronger dollar rather than a stronger yen. \par \par \uc2  Zembei Mizoguchi, head of the Finance Ministry's international bureau, said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if necessary. Mizoguchi's comments, coupled with remarks by other key officials on Monday, kept dealers from selling the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "A fall by the dollar below 120 yen looks excessive to them for now, making dollar-buying easier," Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Kato said. \par \par \uc2  The greenback has twice bounced back from lows below 120 yen in the past few days. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar seems to have hit a sort of near-term double-bottom, so I think it will be firm," said Masahiro Ishikawa, vice president at Bank One's Tokyo branch. \par \par \uc2  Ishikawa added that Japan's bleak economic outlook could eventually take its toll on the yen, although the market was not now paying much attention to the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The market will turn to economic fundamentals in the end," he said. "And it will notice that the Japanese economy is worse than the U.S. economy." \par \par \uc2  Tokyo's benchmark Nikkei share average dipped on Tuesday below the key 10,000 level for the first time since October 10. \par \par \uc2  The dollar reacted little to news reports that Afghan opposition Northern Alliance forces had entered Kabul, Afghanistan's capital. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the news was not necessarily positive for the dollar because of uncertainty surrounding the balance of power in war-torn Afghanistan if the ruling Taliban regime is toppled. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0317 GMT. All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 \par \par \uc2  GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                        Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2000 Cls\par                                 12 Nov.\par                        -----------------------------------------\par  Euro/dlr              0.8917   0.8942   -0.28    -6.10   0.9496\par  Dlr/yen               120.92   120.40   +0.43    +5.65   114.45\par  Euro/yen              107.80   107.65   +0.14    -0.84   108.71\par  Dlr/swiss             1.6443   1.6365   +0.48    +2.69   1.6012\par  Stg/dlr               1.4524   1.4540   -0.11    -3.17   1.5000\par  Can/dlr               1.6028   1.6026   +0.01    +7.47   1.4914\par  Aus/dlr               0.5200   0.5205   -0.10    -7.04   0.5594\par  Euro/swiss            1.4664   1.4633   +0.21    -3.66   1.5221\par  Euro/stg              0.6134   0.6146   -0.20    -3.02   0.6325.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011113dxbd007eq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
