\par\par \b \uc2  NY Air Crash Adds Uncertainty To Year-End Trades \b0\par\par\uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 864 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 November 2001\par 21:00\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  By John Hardy \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- While Monday's air crash in New York immediately raised fears of renewed terrorist activity and carried a short-term threat for the dollar, it hasn't so far caused analysts to back off their trading recommendations for the remaining weeks of the year. \par \par \uc2  However, this new incident has reinforced the view - in place since Sept.11 - that unpredictable market shocks are an ever-present threat to any carefully calculated strategy. \par \par \uc2  "We're in times of great uncertainty," said Philip Shaw, trading advisor at Investec Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  As Shaw acknowledges, a new threat to the dollar will be affected by "whether (Monday's) events are (caused by) terrorists," but, in any case, "it will damage confidence in the dollar for the short-term - and the dollar is probably a sell," he said. \par \par \uc2  However, Shaw's trading perspective is not seriously impacted as he was already advising clients to be positioned for a weak dollar. \par \par \uc2  "We tend to the opinion that the euro will stage a bit of a recovery towards the end of the year - and (Monday's) event has reinforced our negativity towards the dollar," he said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts' recognize that committing to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions has not been rewarding in recent weeks - with major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  unable to break out of narrow ranges - despite an astonishing range of normally market-moving developments, including inconsistent economic data on both sides of the Atlantic, a stream of interest rate cuts from the Federal Reserve and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and of course, unparalleled worldwide political uncertainty created by terrorist activities and the ongoing U.S. response. \par \par \uc2  However, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets have shown remarkable stability in the weeks since Sept. 11, with the euro doing no better than holding in a 5-cent range between $0.88 and $0.93. \par \par \uc2  The yen has been even more difficult to call, trading in a wider range - but failing to follow through convincingly - on either side of a six-week old Y120-Y123 trading range. \par \par \uc2  Late afternoon in New York, the euro was at $0.8948, up from $0.8925 in London but little changed from $0.8942 late Friday in New York. The dollar was at Y120.31, below Y120.76 in London and down a touch from Y120.38 late Friday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at CHF1.6373, close to CHF1.6375 Friday in New York. Sterling was at $1.4574, down from $1.4565 late Friday in New York. \par \par \uc2  However, for Mitul Kotecha, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Credit Agricole Indosuez in London, the market's immediate reaction to Monday's bad news was in sharp contrast to his medium-term view. \par \par \uc2  "The immediate reaction (to the New York air crash) is a flight to safety - benefiting Swissy and the yen," said Kotecha. \par \par \uc2  But, despite this knee-jerk yen buying, Kotecha maintains his expectation that dollar/yen will now start to meet the market's bullish expectations. \par \par \uc2  "We look to dollar/yen to move higher before year-end," said Kotecha, who believes that short-term gains by the yen have actually increased the likelihood of a reversal in favor of the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "We're now back to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels (around Y120)," said Kotecha, "and clearly the yen is overvalued at these levels." \par \par \uc2  In addition to the disincentive to hold yen - generated by the threat of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - Kotecha thinks that capital flows out of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  are now likely to be dollar-positive and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders are no longer interested in utilizing yen to finance other positions. \par \par \uc2  "They have been unwinding the yen carry-trade as interest rate differentials have narrowed," he said. \par \par \uc2  Traders also point out that it's not just the dollar that has lacked direction since Sept. 11, with the normally volatile euro/yen cross largely trading in a narrow 3-yen range. \par \par \uc2  However, in the past week, there have been signs that the euro was starting to lose ground, slipping briefly below Y107 Friday. \par \par \uc2  Not content with just buying dollar/yen, Kotecha would also trade against the downtrend in euro/yen and, he would advise going long euros on the cross, expecting outflows of capital from Japan to allow the pair to return to recent highs around Y110. \par \par \uc2  Taking a different perspective on the relative merits of the euro and the yen, Bob Sinche, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at CitiFX Views, recommends being short the euro. \par \par \uc2  In a current client report, Sinche explains this recommendation by suggesting that the, "unwinding of IMM (futures) positions may continue to weigh on the euro (and) concerns about notes and coins conversion (January 2002) may also limit near-term bottom fishing." As a result of these euro-negative factors, Sinche expects the euro/yen pair to target a seven-week low at Y106.6. \par \par \uc2  On the other hand, he doesn't advise any dollar/yen position for the period to year-end, as he expects the pair to hover inconclusively around the Y120 level. \par \par \uc2  -John Hardy, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2122; john.hardy@dowjones \par \par \uc2  1074 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020011112dxbc008y9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
