\par\par \b \uc2  Asia Markets-Shares mixed, China WTO entry has muted impact. \b0\par\par\uc2      By Ovais Subhani \par \uc2 1,030 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 November 2001\par 09:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2      SINGAPORE, Nov 12 (Reuters) - Asia equity markets were \par \par \uc2  mixed on Monday with Tokyo giving up its early gains while \par \par \uc2  technology stocks led a rise in Korea and Taiwan. \par \par \uc2      A steadier yen helped most regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2   against the \par \par \uc2  dollar, but their upsides were checked on fears central banks \par \par \uc2  may not allow any significant exchange rate appreciation. \par \par \uc2      The dollar was stuck just above the 120 yen level, wedged \par \par \uc2  in between offers from Japanese exporters and fears of possible \par \par \uc2  dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities. A holiday \par \par \uc2  in the United States also kept players from taking aggressive \par \par \uc2  positions. \par \par \uc2      Japan's top financial diplomat, Haruhiko Kuroda, said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  would consider further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold the yen down, after \par \par \uc2  it rose in the past couple of weeks from lows around 123 \par \par \uc2  against the dollar. \par \par \uc2      The Nikkei average ended down 1.31 percent at 10,081.56, as \par \par \uc2  the market focus shifted from the high tech to the earnings \par \par \uc2  woes of the traditional stocks. \par \par \uc2      Hong Kong stocks moved both ways on absence of fresh leads \par \par \uc2  from the U.S. markets and as China plays gave a muted response \par \par \uc2  to the already discounted news of China's entry to the World \par \par \uc2  Trade Organisation. \par \par \uc2      AN ARDUOUS TASK \par \par \uc2      The terms of its entry were approved on Saturday in Doha, \par \par \uc2  Qatar, and by the end of the year China is expected to complete \par \par \uc2  the formalities and become a signatory of the global trading \par \par \uc2  system the WTO represents. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Dai Xianglong said China faced an \par \par \uc2  "arduous task" in reforming its financial systems to respond to \par \par \uc2  the membership. \par \par \uc2      China would push reforms of state banks to help them \par \par \uc2  compete internationally, while improving the exchange rate \par \par \uc2  regime to help cushion external shocks expected after entering \par \par \uc2  the WTO next month, Dai said. \par \par \uc2      He said the government would move towards the yuan's full \par \par \uc2  convertibility with "steady steps". \par \par \uc2      However, most analysts said with China's hands full with \par \par \uc2  urgent financial reforms, especially in the bad loan-riddled \par \par \uc2  banking sector, it could be five years before it turns its \par \par \uc2  attention towards changing its exchange rate policy. \par \par \uc2  The Chinese renminbi, or yuan is only convertible on the \par \par \uc2  current account and the government allows its trade in a \par \par \uc2  domestic spot market, but only within a narrow 8.2770-8.2800 \par \par \uc2  range against the dollar. \par \par \uc2      Over the last few weeks, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has allowed the \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen a few basis points above the band. \par \par \uc2      "The next several years will be the crucial period of \par \par \uc2  China's financial reforms," Dai wrote in the Communist Party \par \par \uc2  flagship People's Daily newspaper. \par \par \uc2      IS CHINA PREPARED? \par \par \uc2      The other areas offering challenge to China and its trading \par \par \uc2  partners would be the impact of changes in import tariffs and \par \par \uc2  investment laws. \par \par \uc2      At the moment China taxes all imported goods at an average \par \par \uc2  16.4 percent rate. This average rate is required to be eased \par \par \uc2  gradually to 10 percent by mid-2006. \par \par \uc2      The average tariff on agriculture products, which \par \par \uc2  constitute a big chunk of China's trade portfolio, are required \par \par \uc2  to be cut to 15.7 percent from 22.7 percent, and import of \par \par \uc2  industrial goods will be taxed at 9.1 percent, down from 16.6 \par \par \uc2  percent. \par \par \uc2  By 2005, China is supposed to allow foreign-affiliated \par \par \uc2  businesses to independently import and export goods rather than \par \par \uc2  pass their orders through Chinese firms. \par \par \uc2  Much before that, China is expected to ease restrictions on \par \par \uc2  foreign direct investment under the WTO agreement on \par \par \uc2  Trade-related Investment Measures. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts fear these changes may put millions of \par \par \uc2  Chinese out of work as poorly managed state-run enterprises may \par \par \uc2  crumble in the face of competition. \par \par \uc2  "The main shocks are going to be to those parts of the \par \par \uc2  Chinese economy which have been on a diet of uncompetitive \par \par \uc2  practices," said Richard Margolis, first vice president of Asia \par \par \uc2  Pacific equities research at Merrill Lynch in Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  But Margolis said that does not necessarily mean China's \par \par \uc2  state-owned sector is incapable of meeting the challenges of \par \par \uc2  the post-WTO era or the restructuring would result in a massive \par \par \uc2  social upheaval. \par \par \uc2  "A large part of the state-owned sector has and is capable \par \par \uc2  to of shaping up...China has already been shutting down large \par \par \uc2  chunks of its uncompetitive industry," he said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  0754 GMT \par \par \uc2   Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen     120.53       120.46         -0.06\par  Sing dlr      1.8255       1.8270         +0.08\par  Taiwan dlr    34.498       34.500         +0.01\par  Korean won   1284.50      1275.00         -0.74\par  Baht           44.37        44.46         +0.20\par  Peso           52.05        51.88         -0.32\par  Rupiah      10650.00     10375.00         -2.58\par  Ringgit       3.7995       3.8000         +0.01\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on year\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2       Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par  Japan yen     120.53       114.45         -5.04\par  Sing dlr      1.8255       1.7365         -4.88\par  Taiwan dlr    34.498       32.900         -4.63\par  Korean won   1284.50      1277.00         -0.58\par  Baht           44.37        43.70         -1.51\par  Peso           52.05        50.75         -2.49\par  Rupiah      10650.00      9470.00        -11.08\par  Ringgit       3.7995       3.8000         +0.01\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  ASIAN STOCK MARKETS  0754 GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on the day\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Market       Current      Prev Close     Pct Move\par  Tokyo       10081.56     10215.71         -1.31\par  Hong Kong   10578.52     10609.25         -0.29\par  Singapore    1369.62      1362.77         +0.50\par  Taipei       4172.63      4123.78         +1.18\par  Seoul         584.48       576.75         +1.34\par  Bangkok       271.53       268.11         +1.28\par  Manila        993.23       999.79         -0.66\par  Jakarta       369.00       377.34         -2.21\p