\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 's Kuroda says further  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  possible. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Gary Cox \par \uc2 468 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 November 2001\par 05:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  CANBERRA, Nov 12 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top financial diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda said on Monday that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would consider further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  "We would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if exchange rates deviated from the economic fundamentals," Kuroda told Reuters outside a meeting on regional financial arrangements in East Asia. \par \par \uc2  "But we do not intend to depreciate or appreciate our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  out of (line with) economic fundamentals. We do respect economic fundamentals and we always closely monitor exchange rates in the market." \par \par \uc2  Kuroda, the vice finance minister for international affairs, said there was no set target level for the exchange rate. "We do not have any specific levels of exchange rate, I don't think any of the developed countries have specific exchange rate levels," he said. \par \par \uc2  The yen retreated from one-month highs to the dollar last week on wariness of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap its strength. \par \par \uc2  This followed comments from Kuroda that recent yen moves were unfavourable, reminding the market that Japan spend over US$26 billion in September to deflect an export-damaging rise. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda declined to comment on Monday on whether the Bank of Japan would loosen its ultra-easy monetary policy further. \par \par \uc2  CALLS FOR AGGRESSION \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan is facing calls to adopt a more aggressive monetary policy to revive the world's second biggest economy, with efforts to pump more money into the economy through the banking system not filtering through. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  eased its monetary policy in August and September in a bid to halt a two-year slide in consumer prices and unprecedented deflation which is threatening a fourth recession this decade. \par \par \uc2  The bank's last eased on September 18 as it cut its symbolic official discount rate to 0.10 percent from 0.25 percent and expanded its emergency Lombard lending facility, joining a global effort to avoid recession after the attacks on the United States. \par \par \uc2  But despite the de facto zero interest rate policy, many politicians and prominent economists continue to argue for further loosening. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda said he could not speculate on the economic impact in Japan from the attacks in the United States when hijacked aircraft slammed into New York and Washington. \par \par \uc2  "I think all of the G7 economies were affected by the September 11 events," he said. \par \par \uc2  "I think the Japanese economy is also affected but the extent of the impact will differ from one economy to another and the basic point is that all the economies in the world are affected by the events of September 11." \par \par \uc2  At 0445 GMT, dollar/yen was quoted at 120.47 in Asia, while euro/yen stood at 107.60, compared with Friday's close in New York of 120.29 and 107.25. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011112dxbc008nc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
