\par\par \b \uc2  Shiokawa says further yen rise unfavourable-paper. \b0\par\par\uc2 327 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 November 2001\par 04:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Nov 11 (Reuters) - Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said a further rise in the yen would be unfavourable for Japan, adding that he had ordered his staff to consider \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a domestic newspaper reported on Sunday. \par \par \uc2  "A further strengthening in the yen would not be good for Japan, such as for Japanese exports," Shiokawa was quoted by the Nihon Keizai Shimbun as saying in a speech in Osaka, western Japan, on Saturday. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa said he had ordered his staff to consider \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market because there had been a possibility of a further rise in the yen in New York trading on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had hovered around 120.30 yen in late New York trading on Friday after touching a low of 119.77 yen on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  A strong yen theoretically makes Japan's exports more expensive on world markets and erodes the yen value of Japanese companies' overseas earnings. \par \par \uc2  Japan's top financial diplomat, Haruhiko Kuroda, said on Friday recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves were unfavourable and did not reflect economic fundamentals. He said he thought the moves were temporary but the ministry was monitoring the market carefully. \par \par \uc2  In late September, the Finance Ministry, using the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  as its agent, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prevent a rise in the yen due to concerns that a rapid strengthening in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could hurt the nation's fragile economy. \par \par \uc2  During that month, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on seven business days from September 17, mostly selling yen and buying dollars in an effort to keep the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from rising in the wake of the September 11 attacks on the United States. \par \par \uc2  On three occasions it bought euros for yen, each time enlisting the help of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It also bought dollars for yen through the New York Federal Reserve on September 27. All \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  were funded by Japan. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011111dxbb00336\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
