\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen retreats from highs, euro remains underdog. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Carolyn Cohn \par \uc2 694 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 November 2001\par 12:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Nov 9 (Reuters) - The yen retreated from this week's one-month highs against the dollar on Friday on wariness of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap its strength, while the euro was suffering after a half-point cut in euro-zone rates on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Japan's top financial diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda said in Tokyo trade recent yen moves were unfavourable, reminding the market of aggressive yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in September. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , meanwhile, failed to convince the market it was tackling the euro zone's economic problems with the same gusto applied to the U.S. economy by the Federal Reserve. \par \par \uc2  "There is a strong chance \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 117-118, so the risk of the dollar falling much below 120 is fairly low," said Steven Saywell, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank. \par \par \uc2  "The 50 basis point rate cut should have been a bullish signal for the euro, but the market still sees the ECB as behind the curve." \par \par \uc2  The dollar gained a quarter percent against the yen from the New York close to test 120.50 by 1045 GMT, three-quarters of a yen above one-month lows set in the previous session. \par \par \uc2  The euro lost a quarter percent to hover around $0.89, after hitting two-week lows of $0.8889 in Asian trade. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  maintained its poise against the yen, however, trading around half a yen above six-week lows of 106.76 set on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Traders attributed some of the euro's weakness in the wake of the unexpectedly large rate cut as down to suspicion the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been pressured by euro zone finance ministers into making the move. \par \par \uc2  A Frankfurt-based trader said, "It won't solve the political problems of euroland. They were forced to do it." \par \par \uc2  WATCH THAT YEN \par \par \uc2  Some traders said yen gains this week had been triggered by Japanese investors taking profits on the recent rally in U.S. Treasuries and converting their dollar gains into yen, while others said the euro's losses across the board were the main driver for yen gains. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also came under pressure against the yen after data showed Japan had a net capital inflow of 175 billion yen in the week of October 29 compared with net outflows of more than one trillion yen in the previous week. \par \par \uc2  But verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Kuroda and from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi reminded the market Japan spent over $26 billion in September to deflect an export-damaging rise. \par \par \uc2  "The market got a gentle reminder Japan will not be happy to see the yen strengthen," John Kyriakopoulos, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at J.P. Morgan Chase said. \par \par \uc2  "We know the Bank of Japan was aggressive in September, so his comment was what people were anticipating once the dollar goes below 120 yen. It's business as usual for the Japanese authorities." \par \par \uc2  Analysts agreed with Japanese policy-makers that the yen's gains were not justified by fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese government said on Friday it expected the economy to shrink nearly one percent in the year to March, its worst contraction in at least 20 years, tossing out an unrealistic forecast of 1.7 percent growth. \par \par \uc2  U.S. SENTIMENT TEST \par \par \uc2  Many dealers expected dollar moves to be limited by a U.S. holiday on Monday. But the risks are greater for the dollar to test the downside, they said. \par \par \uc2  The market was focused on the U.S. October producer prices index at 1330 GMT, forecast to show a drop of 0.4 percent from the previous month's 0.4 percent gain, and the University of Michigan November consumer sentiment survey at 1500 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Another drop in U.S. consumer confidence was likely to pressure U.S. equities and prompt investors to unwind their dollar positions ahead of the weekend, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The University of Michigan's closely watched consumer sentiment index is expected to show a decline in confidence in November after a surprise uptick last month. Economists polled by Reuters see the index dropping to 78.7 in November from 82.7 in October. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011109dxb900lrt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
