\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  3Q  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  Shows Strong Intent To Cap Yen \b0\par\par\uc2  By Arran Scott \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 501 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 November 2001\par 07:28\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market reached record proportions in September, as the government battled to prevent a strong yen from dealing a death blow to the faltering economy. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Wednesday that it sold Y3.211 trillion for dollars and euros in 10 rounds of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted through the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  between Sept. 17-28. Japan sold Y3.146 trillion against the dollar and Y65.2 billion against the euro, according to data for the July-September quarter. \par \par \uc2  The aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  effort came after the Sept. 11 terrorist attacks in the U.S. precipitated a sharp plunge in the dollar that eventually pushed the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down to a low of Y115.80 on Sept. 20. Over time, the yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  lifted the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back up to around Y119.30 - shy of the Y122 level where it traded before the attacks. \par \par \uc2  Around 0600 GMT Wednesday, the dollar was quoted at Y120.80. \par \par \uc2  "They were aggressive... the most aggressive they've ever been," said Kenneth Landon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  underlines the ministry's strong intent to put a floor under the dollar and prevent a strong yen from stifling the struggling economy by undermining the competitiveness of exporters selling in overseas markets. \par \par \uc2  Even before the Sept. 11 attacks cast a pall over consumer demand in the U.S., Japanese exports were in a declining trend. A strengthening yen on top of slackening demand would likely have had a severe impact on manufacturers, which have already slashed production for three straight quarters through September in response to weakening demand. \par \par \uc2  Concerns about the outlook for the U.S. economy continue to weigh on the dollar. Rate cuts by the Federal Reserve have brightened prospects somewhat, but few market participants are willing to bet on a quick recovery, traders say. \par \par \uc2  Landon said he expects the dollar to drift lower "but not that much," with buying-demand likely to support the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  above Y120. \par \par \uc2  Few market participants doubt Japanese authorities would act again if the yen strengthens suddenly. But whether they take action would depends on the speed of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, with sharp moves more likely to be countered by swift action, Landon said. \par \par \uc2  Wednesday's data show that the weighted average of the ministry's dollar purchases in September was around Y117.50, making that a key level markets participants will keep an eye on, Landon said. \par \par \uc2  "The mid-117s is generally a crucial level," Landon said. \par \par \uc2  Separately, the ministry said its reserves of convertible foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , gold and International Monetary Fund special drawing rights totaled $405.7 billion in October, up $8.7 billion from the previous month. That followed a rise of Y24.79 billion in September. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Arran Scott, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par arran.scott@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011107dxb7000bp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
