\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar feels heat in wake of Fed cut, euro glows. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Dhara Ranasinghe \par \uc2 715 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 November 2001\par 14:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Nov 7 (Reuters) - The dollar fell one percent against the euro and Swiss franc on Wednesday as worries grew that the Federal Reserve is running out of ammunition to boost the fragile U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  The Fed on Tuesday delivered its 10th rate cut of the year, lowering its key rate half a point to a 40-year low. But with worries creeping in over how much more the Fed can do to boost growth, the dollar reversed its initial post-rate cut gains and fell to 2-1/2 week lows versus the yen. \par \par \uc2  Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , awaiting an interest rate decision from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday, rallied more than half a percent against the yen. It was helped by talk China may be set to increase its euro reserves by 10 percent although Chinese authorities declined to comment on the matter. \par \par \uc2  "After the initial (U.S. rate cut) news was digested, there was a sentiment entering the market that the impact of interest rate reductions was starting to run out of steam," said Harley Dale, international economist at Westpac in London. \par \par \uc2  "There is not a lot left that the Fed can do," he added. \par \par \uc2  At 1245 GMT, the dollar hovered just above the day's lows around $0.9047 per euro and 1.6260 Swiss francs. It earlier fell to 2-1/2 week troughs around 120.75 yen. \par \par \uc2  The euro stood around 109.05 yen, up more than half a percent on the day with dealers citing speculation that China could raise its euro reserves as a reason behind euro gains. \par \par \uc2  China does not comment on the composition of its reserves although the bulk is believed to be held in U.S. dollars. \par \par \uc2  The euro showed little immediate reaction to news German September industry orders fell 4.1 percent, against market expectations of a 2.2 percent decline. \par \par \uc2  U.S. GROWTH WORRIES MOUNT \par \par \uc2  Worries over U.S. growth were reflected in weakness in U.S. stock futures, further souring sentiment towards the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The initial knee-jerk reaction to the Fed's rate cut was positive for stocks and the dollar," said Paul Mackel, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein in Frankfurt. \par \par \uc2  "But you have to start wondering when all this stimulus will help the economy and this is one of the reasons why the dollar has come under broad pressure today." \par \par \uc2  The interest rate differential between the U.S. and the euro zone were also seen working against the greenback. \par \par \uc2  Tuesday's rate cut puts euro zone benchmark interest rates at almost double the level of their U.S. counterparts, raising the appeal of euro-denominated assets. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that weakness in the price of oil, which is bought and sold in dollars, was also a cause of concern for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Oil prices on Wednesday hovered around their lowest level in two years below $20 per barrel. \par \par \uc2  "A fall in oil prices to around $19 per barrel is not supportive for the dollar," said Matthew Clements, economist at Prebon Marshall Yamane. \par \par \uc2  ON THE AGENDA \par \par \uc2  Dealers were awaiting third quarter U.S. productivity and unit labour cost data at 1330 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Beyond this, the focus remained on Thursday's ECB interest rate announcement, with markets expecting a quarter point cut. \par \par \uc2  ECB council member Ernst Welteke said earlier the global economy was in a phase of pronounced weakness and uncertainty but he was "cautiously optimistic" the euro zone would recover in the medium term. \par \par \uc2  The yen, meanwhile, failed to benefit from dollar weakness with some focus on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the day, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) said it had spent 3.2 trillion yen ($26.42 billion) on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the July-September quarter, a record amount for a quarter. \par \par \uc2  That aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  pushed the dollar from lows around 115.80 yen in late September to a high above 123 yen hit in late October. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was little moved by brewing financial turmoil in Argentina, despite the United States' close economic links with the country. Argentina's government unveiled a debt-restructuring plan on Tuesday that it hoped would revive its troubled economy. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011107dxb700op4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
