\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar under fire after Fed cut, euro shines. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Dhara Ranasinghe \par \uc2 676 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 November 2001\par 12:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Nov 7 (Reuters) - The dollar shed one percent against the euro and Swiss franc on Wednesday as doubts emerged over whether the previous day's aggressive cut in U.S. interest rates would help the frail U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  The Federal Reserve delivered its 10th rate cut of the year, lowering its key rate half a point to a 40-year low. But with worries creeping in over how much more the Fed can do to boost growth, the dollar reversed its post-rate cut gains and fell to 2-1/2 week lows versus the yen. \par \par \uc2  Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  outshone its counterparts, rallying more than half a percent against the yen, helped by talk that China may be set to increase its euro reserves by 10 percent. Chinese authorities declined to comment on the matter. \par \par \uc2  "The initial knee-jerk reaction to the Fed's rate cut was positive for stocks and the dollar," said Paul Mackel, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein in Frankfurt. \par \par \uc2  "But you have to start wondering when all this stimulus will help the economy and this is one of the reasons why the dollar has come under broad pressure today." \par \par \uc2  At 1115 GMT, the dollar stood just above the day's lows around $0.9047 per euro and 1.6260 Swiss francs. It earlier fell to 2-1/2 week troughs around 120.75 yen. \par \par \uc2  The euro stood around 109.20 yen, up more than half a percent on the day with dealers citing speculation that China could raise its euro reserves as a reason behind euro gains. \par \par \uc2  China never comments on the composition of its reserves although the bulk is believed to be held in U.S. dollars. \par \par \uc2  The euro gave little immediate reaction to data showing that German September industry orders fell 4.1 percent, a much steeper fall than analysts' forecasts of a 2.2 percent decline. \par \par \uc2  U.S. WORRIES GROW \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the interest rate differential between the U.S. and the euro zone could begin to work against the greenback. \par \par \uc2  "The Fed's decision is weighing on the market and dealers can't help but feel the U.S. is moving towards the situation that Japan finds itself in," said Steve Barrow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bear Stearns. \par \par \uc2  "There's also a feeling that the Fed can't continue to cut rates and not have a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he added. \par \par \uc2  The federal funds rate now stands at 2.0 percent, 1.75 percentage points below the euro zone's benchmark rate. \par \par \uc2  U.S. equity futures suggested the likelihood of a rocky start at the U.S. open on Wednesday, further souring sentiment towards the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The weakness of the stocks reflects worries about the global economy, or more precisely, the U.S. economy. It suggests that the 50 basis point cut was not really inspirational for confidence. This is hitting the dollar too," said one trader. \par \par \uc2  Attention was also on Thursday's European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate announcement with European Monetary Affairs Commissioner Pedro Solbes saying Europe should not by necessity apply the same monetary policy as in the United States. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  THREAT LINGERS \par \par \uc2  The Japanese yen failed to benefit from dollar weakness. \par \par \uc2  Though Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa has been silent on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels recently, the Japanese government is still seen as ready to step into the market if dollar/yen slips through the 120 level. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the day, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) said it had spent 3.2 trillion yen ($26.42 billion) on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the July-September quarter, a record amount for a quarter. \par \par \uc2  That aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  pushed the dollar from lows around 115.80 yen in late September to a high above 123 yen hit in late October. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was little moved by brewing financial turmoil in Argentina, despite the United States' close economic links with the country. Argentina's government unveiled a debt-restructuring plan on Tuesday that it hoped would revive its troubled economy. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011107dxb700lgg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
