\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar mired near lows on U.S. economic gloom. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christina Fincher \par \uc2 651 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2001\par 12:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Oct 31 (Reuters) - The dollar was mired near recent multi-week lows against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday on nervousness that upcoming third-quarter U.S. growth data would cast further gloom over the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  After recovering to pre-September 11 levels last week on optimism of an eventual U.S. rebound, the dollar has turned tail this week, hitting two-week lows on the euro and three-week lows on the Swiss franc on Tuesday after news of a sharp drop in U.S. consumer confidence. \par \par \uc2  Economists polled by Reuters predict the U.S. economy shrank by 1.0 percent in the third quarter. This would be its worst performance in a decade. \par \par \uc2  "The market has been front-running what is expected to be a series of very weak U.S. data," said Paul Mackel, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein. \par \par \uc2  "The risks to the data are to the downside, which could weigh on the dollar." \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Conference Board said on Tuesday its consumer confidence index fell more than expected to a seven year low in October. This dealt a blow to U.S. stocks and soured sentiment towards the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The dollar trading near day's lows at 121.75 yen at 1105 GMT, within sight of Tuesday's one-week lows. The dollar stood at $0.9050 per euro, half a cent from two-week lows set the previous session. \par \par \uc2  "People are fearful of a horrendous GDP number after recent shocks," said Rob Hayward, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro in London. \par \par \uc2  ECB POLICY EYED \par \par \uc2  The prospect for monetary easing in the euro zone remained in focus after European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Vice President Christian Noyer was quoted in Italian newspaper Il Sole 24 Ore as saying the ECB would consider adjusting interest rates if the inflation outlook changed. \par \par \uc2  ECB chief economist Otmar Issing late on Tuesday conceded the euro zone faced a poor third quarter and there was no prospect of the fourth quarter being better. \par \par \uc2  Issing declined to comment on the possible outcome of the ECB policymaking council meeting next week but stressed the bank "will move quickly and powerfully when the time is right". \par \par \uc2  Dealers said such comments could support the euro if they were taken as a hint the bank would not only cut rates next week but ease by a full 50 basis points. \par \par \uc2  "Lower interest rates in the euro zone should be positive for the euro but any upside is limited as a rate cut in early November is priced into the market," said Ryan Shea, international economist at Bank One in London. \par \par \uc2  "The dominant driver for the euro this week is weak U.S. data and the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should have a good spell." \par \par \uc2  Holding the euro back on Wednesday, however, was news that French unemployment rose for a fourth consecutive month in September. \par \par \uc2  The euro pulled back from earlier three-week peaks against the yen to around 110.25 yen , little changed on the day. \par \par \uc2  WATCHING THE YEN \par \par \uc2  Traders said the market may test the dollar down towards 121 yen in the near term, but no steep falls were expected amid caution over any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Japanese authorities. \par \par \uc2  Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  director Masayuki Matsushima said on Wednesday there was no intention to deliberately weaken the yen but equally there was no reason for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise in the current climate. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials have made it clear they do not want the yen to appreciate, and so hurt exports, though the BOJ has always resisted calls to actively push it much lower. \par \par \uc2  In any case dealers doubted dollar/yen would budge much near term given talk of massive option expiries around 122.00 on Wednesday and through the week. \par \par \uc2  Option positions were being defended under 121.50 and above 122.30, they added. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011031dxav00k2k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
