\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar on defensive ahead of U.S. growth data. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Dhara Ranasinghe \par \uc2 772 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2001\par 10:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Oct 31 (Reuters) - The dollar remained on the back foot on Wednesday on wariness that U.S. third-quarter growth data due later in the day would provide further proof of a frail economy. \par \par \uc2  Economists polled by Reuters expect, on average, the U.S. economy shrank by 1.0 percent in the third quarter, which would be its worst performance in a decade. \par \par \uc2  Such news would add to a stream of recent weak economic data that has hurt the dollar, with the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  falling to multi-week lows against the euro, yen and Swiss franc on Tuesday on news of a sharp drop in U.S. consumer sentiment. \par \par \uc2  "People are fearful of a horrendous GDP number after recent shocks," said Rob Hayward, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro in London. \par \par \uc2  "They are also looking ahead to this week's employment data and this is keeping the dollar on the defensive." \par \par \uc2  The U.S. non-farm payrolls numbers due out on Friday are seen as a key indicator of U.S. economic health. \par \par \uc2  At 0835 GMT, the dollar stood around $0.9060 per euro, down slightly from late New York levels and within half a percent of two-week troughs set a day earlier. \par \par \uc2  End-month commercial demand lifted the dollar to an intraday high of 122.22 yen during Asian trade, but the greenback slowly drifted down to around 121.80 yen in early Europe. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Conference Board, a private research group, said on Tuesday its consumer confidence index tumbled 11.5 points to 85.5 in October, its lowest level in more than seven years. \par \par \uc2  That dealt a blow to U.S. stocks and further soured sentiment towards the dollar, dealers said. The benchmark Dow Jones industrial average closed more than 1.5 percent lower, putting in its poorest showing in about three weeks. \par \par \uc2  SPOTLIGHT ON ECB \par \par \uc2  The euro rallied to three-week peaks around 110.70 yen, before trimming gains to around 110.30 yen, little changed on the day. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was some focus on comments by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) Vice President Christian Noyer, who was quoted in Italian newspaper Il Sole 24 Ore as saying the ECB would consider adjusting interest rates if the inflation outlook changed. \par \par \uc2  ECB chief economist Otmar Issing late on Tuesday conceded the euro zone faced a poor third quarter and there was no prospect of the fourth quarter being better. \par \par \uc2  Issing declined to comment on the possible outcome of the ECB policymaking council meeting next week but stressed the bank "will move quickly and powerfully when the time is right". \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the comment could help support the euro if it was taken as a hint the bank would not only cut rates next week but ease by a full 50 basis points. \par \par \uc2  "Lower interest rates in the euro zone should be positive for the euro but any upside is limited as a rate cut in early November is priced into the market," said Ryan Shea, international economist at Bank One in London. \par \par \uc2  "The dominant driver for the euro this week is weak U.S. data and the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should have a good spell." \par \par \uc2  WATCHING THE YEN \par \par \uc2  Traders said the market may test the dollar down towards 121 yen in the near term, but no steep falls were expected amid caution over any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Japanese authorities. \par \par \uc2  Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  director Masayuki Matsushima said on Wednesday there was no intention to deliberately weaken the yen but equally there was no reason for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise in the current climate. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials have made it clear they do not want the yen to appreciate, and so hurt exports, though the BOJ has always resisted calls to actively push it much lower. \par \par \uc2  In any case dealers doubted dollar/yen would budge much near term given talk of massive option expiries around 122.00 on Wednesday and through the week. Option positions were being defended under 121.50 and above 122.30, they added. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, sterling trimmed recent gains after the Gfk Consumer Confidence Barometer, released early Wednesday, fell by much more than expected to -5 in October from -1 the month before and a peak of +6 in June. \par \par \uc2  The survey found a marked deterioration in confidence about the UK economy's prospects with the expectations balance for next year diving 13 points to -33, its lowest level since September 1990. \par \par \uc2  The news kept the pound restrained around $1.4535 after hitting a three-week high around $1.4600 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011031dxav00ffw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
