\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  -2: Eurozone Econ Concerns Keep A Lid On Euro \b0\par\par\uc2 300 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Oktober 2001\par 06:50\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  The Japanese authorities are likely serious when they hint they will take action, partly because they need to keep yen strength from crushing the weak domestic economy, but also because \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  provide a means of supporting the dollar, a trader at a Japanese city bank said. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are preventing a massive outflow of funds from the U.S., and can be considered as being a financial form of 'rear support'" Tokyo has promised to provide Washington in its war against terrorism, the trader said. \par \par \uc2  The euro remained steady against the dollar after regaining the 90 cent-level in New York, although it was capped by lingering concerns over Eurozone fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  "It's difficult to believe European economies are that much better off than the U.S. or Japan," said another European bank trader, pointing out that U.S. indicators precede those in the Eurozone. \par \par \uc2  "Though we're not aware of them now, we may gradually start seeing some poor European numbers down the line," he said. \par \par \uc2  Moreover, the market's distrust of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which many believe to be behind the curve in implementing monetary policy, hasn't yet been dispelled, Tokyo traders said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Sterling was at $1.4553, up from $1.4527 in New York and $1.4382 in\par Tokyo Monday.\par   -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par miyako.takebe@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The chart of late