\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro slides after ECB keeps interest rates on hold. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Robin Shepherd \par \uc2 573 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Oktober 2001\par 14:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Oct 25 (Reuters) - The euro fell a quarter of a percent against the dollar and the yen on Thursday after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left interest rates unchanged, hastening a decline begun earlier as a result of U.S. equity gains. \par \par \uc2  The euro hit an intraday low of $0.8880 after the ECB decision, putting it tantalisingly close to seven-week lows at $0.8865 set on Tuesday. The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stands down around two thirds of a percent against the dollar from the New York close. "This is bad news for the euro," said Sonja Hellemann, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein in London. \par \par \uc2  "The perception is that the ECB should cut and could cut, and that they are not doing enough to deal with the economic downturn." \par \par \uc2  Market views on an interest rate decision had been mixed, with 27 out of 50 economists polled by Reuters favouring a rate cut, given weak recent economic data and Wednesday's benign German inflation figures. \par \par \uc2  The dollar, boosted by Wednesday's equity price gains on Wall Street, consolidated gains against the yen. It hit a 2-1/2 month high at 123.36 yen earlier in the session. \par \par \uc2  ECB AND GROWTH \par \par \uc2  The ECB has cut rates by 100 basis points so far this year, compared with the U.S. Federal Reserve's 400 basis points. \par \par \uc2  Some economists said the ECB may have been reluctant to act because it wanted to avoid the appearance of bowing to pressure from eurozone politicians who are urging monetary easing to stimulate growth. \par \par \uc2  The German government on Thursday revised its 2001 growth forecast downward to around 0.75 percent from around two percent and cut its target for next year to around 1.25 percent from 2.25 percent. \par \par \uc2  The German finance ministry also forecast inflation for both this year and next would be two percent and said it saw exports growing by 5.5 percent this year and four percent in 2002. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said optimism that aggressive U.S. rate cuts and plans for heavy fiscal stimulus would revive U.S. domestic growth had attracted capital from the rest of the world, bolstering Wall Street and the dollar. \par \par \uc2  SOFT YEN \par \par \uc2  Comments from Japanese officials may be weighing on the yen, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Japan's top financial diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda told a Japanese television programme late on Wednesday it was inappropriate for the yen to strengthen and that Japan was watching the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly in recent weeks to prevent its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from strengthening and thus hurting exports. \par \par \uc2  Some took Kuroda's comments as a green light to sell the yen further, despite worries that too rapid and substantial a fall in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could cause friction with Japan's Asian trading partners. \par \par \uc2  BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami told the Japanese Upper House financial committee the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had no plans to adopt an inflation-targeting policy at this point. \par \par \uc2  "Comments from Kuroda and Hayami have refocused attention on the lack of policy options available to kick start the economy," a Japanese bank dealer in London said. \par \par \uc2  Japanese capital flow data on Thursday showed investors stepped up their foreign bond purchases - especially U.S. dollar bonds - for the week to last Friday, buying 1.189 trillion yen ($9.66 billion) on hopes of more U.S. rate cuts. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011025dxap00ovh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
