\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar extends rally as US outlook brightens. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chikafumi Hodo \par \uc2 988 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Oktober 2001\par 08:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Oct 23 (Reuters) - The dollar extended its broad-based gains in late Tokyo on Tuesday after bouncing to pre-attack levels offshore on a growing view that the United States will be the first to recover from the global economic downturn. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said a policy mix of aggressive rate cuts by the Federal Reserve and heavy fiscal stimulus has helped restore faith in the U.S. economy, despite the likelihood that the September 11 attacks pushed it into recession. \par \par \uc2  The turnaround in dollar sentiment comes against a backdrop of deepening economic gloom in Europe and Japan. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar rose on a broad (price) correction after a month of bearish sentiment," said Kosuke Hanao, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at Royal Bank of Scotland. \par \par \uc2  "From the macro-economic point of view, it's still difficult to aggressively buy the dollar, but with the swift monetary and fiscal action from the U.S. authorities and quick restructuring measures by corporations, the market seems to have started expecting a faster economic recovery," Hanao said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell to below 116 yen in the aftermath of the deadly attacks and remained fragile despite repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary authorities to weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  Late bids from European operators pushed the dollar above its offshore high of 122.60 yen in late Tokyo trade. \par \par \uc2  At 0618 GMT, the dollar stood at 122.62/67 yen against Monday's late U.S. level of 122.49/57 yen. \par \par \uc2  It rose as high as 122.70 yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's upswing may gather momentum if it breaks above 122.80 yen, where a clutch of stop-loss orders is rumoured to have been placed. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is unlikely to jump suddenly, but the downside is gradually popping up with the 121 yen level looking very firm now," said Junya Tanase, global markets officer at Chase Manhattan Bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  research division. \par \par \uc2  JAPAN INVESTORS \par \par \uc2  The dollar was also getting some support against the yen as Japanese banks and institutional investors showed strong interest in buying foreign bonds, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Japanese capital flow data indicate that domestic investors flocked into foreign bonds in recent weeks. \par \par \uc2  Frustrated by rock-bottom interest rate at home, Japanese investors have been buyers of foreign bonds since the start of the second half of the fiscal year on October 1. \par \par \uc2  According to \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data, Japanese investors have bought a total of 2.26 trillion yen worth of foreign bonds since the start of the month. They also bought 827.2 billion yen worth in the final week of September. \par \par \uc2  Still, the impact on the dollar/yen rate has not been as great as the figures might suggest since investors have fully hedged most of the bond purchases through forward \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  contracts. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese investors continue to be showing interest in buying dollar-and euro-denominated bonds, but the impact on spot yen trade has been limited because most of them are fully hedged," a Japanese trust bank trader said. "Investors simply don't want to take \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks." \par \par \uc2  Investors are mainly using three-and six-month long-term forward contracts to hedge their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk. \par \par \uc2  With the cost of using \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  forwards falling sharply in recent months, reflecting a series of U.S. interest rate cuts, investors are keen to maximise income from U.S. bond investments without worrying about \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility. \par \par \uc2  Three-month dollar/yen forward contracts were at a discount of 0.70 yen to the spot rate on Monday. This compares with a discount of around 1.6 yen in January. \par \par \uc2  EURO MIRED \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $0.8912/13 , above an offshore low of $0.8895, but was still looking vulnerable. Dealers said the euro could soon test $0.8843, a low hit on September 4. \par \par \uc2  On top of bearish economic forecasts from Germany's six leading economic institutes, dealers said comments by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials continued to undermine confidence in the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke said on Monday the euro was probably undervalued against the dollar and sterling, but reiterated that the ECB did not target the exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  "Central bankers always like a strong external value for their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Welteke said. "(But) The exchange rate is not the goal of monetary policy. Stability is." \par \par \uc2  Germany's six leading economic institutes said the country was on the brink of recession. They slashed their forecasts for growth to 0.7 percent in 2001 and 1.3 percent in 2002, well below April's forecasts of 2.1 and 2.2 percent respectively. \par \par \uc2  The report fanned slim hopes that the ECB would cut rates when it meets this Thursday. However, some analysts said the bank could wait until its next meeting on November 8 to ease. \par \par \uc2  Welteke's recent comment that the ECB's September 17 rate cut was an extraordinary measure, and that the bank's monetary policy was returning to normal, was still weighing on the euro, dealers said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0606 GMT. All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 \par \par \uc2  GMT. \par \par \uc2  Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2000 Cls \par \par \uc2  22 Oct. \par \par \uc2  ----------------------------------------- \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro/dlr              0.8918   0.8920   -0.02    -6.09   0.9496\par Dlr/yen               122.57   122.42   +0.12    +7.09   114.45\par Euro/yen              109.32   109.23   +0.08    +0.56   108.71\par Dlr/swiss             1.6604   1.6588   +0.10    +3.70   1.6012\par Stg/dlr               1.4248   1.4253   -0.04    -5.01   1.5000\par Can/dlr               1.5794   1.5783   +0.07    +5.90   1.4914\par Aus/dlr               0.5096   0.5105   -0.18    -8.90   0.5594\par Euro/swiss            1.4807   1.4782   +0.17    -2.72   1.5221\par Euro/stg              0.6254   0.6251   +0.05    -1.12   0.6325.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011023dxan00bqt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
