\par\par \b \uc2  Japan Shiokawa: Econ Contraction Unavoidable -LDP Kyuma \b0\par\par\uc2 158 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Oktober 2001\par 04:24\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Japan must weaken the yen a little more, Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa told a top policy maker, Kyodo News reported Thursday, suggesting the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  still believes the yen is too strong. \par \par \uc2  A strong yen threatens to hurt Japan's ailing economy by depressing exports and intensifying deflation. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa also told Liberal Democratic Party deputy policy chief Fumio Kyuma that a contraction in the economy is unavoidable, according to Nikkei News. \par \par \uc2  The Finance Ministry, which sets \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively last month to weaken the yen after the Sept. 11 terrorist attacks in the U.S. precipitated a plunge in the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The ministry succeeded in jacking the dollar back above Y120 from around Y116. Thursday at 0255 GMT, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading around Y120.95. \par \par \uc2  Haruhiko Kuroda, Shiokawa's deputy, said earlier Thursday he was closely monitoring the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011018dxai0004q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
