\par\par \b \uc2  Rubin: Weak Yen Not Answer To Japan's Econ Problems \b0\par\par\uc2 942 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Oktober 2001\par 03:11\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- A weaker yen won't solve Japan's deep economic problems, former U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin said Wednesday, adding that advocating a weak yen as the solution to Japan's malaise could actually do more harm than good. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think a weak yen is the answer to Japan's economic problems," Rubin told a gathering hosted by the Japan society and Salomon Smith Barney. "I think the answer to Japan's economic problems lies in the area of structural reform, things like open credit markets and dealing with fiscal problems." \par \par \uc2  In the weeks since Sept. 11, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been extremely active in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly to weaken the yen against both the dollar and the euro. In doing so, analysts say the BOJ - acting on the order of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  - sent a clear message that it would prefer to see the dollar above Y120 against the yen. Many Japanese policy makers advocate a weaker yen, arguing that it will give a much needed boost to Japanese exporters and help stimulate the overall economy. \par \par \uc2  Rubin appeared to dispute. "I'm not saying the weak yen might not have a short-term effect ... but it is actually harmful to think of the weak yen as an answer to Japan's problems because it could deter people from facing the issues they need to face, which are the hard issues." \par \par \uc2  Rubin reaffirmed much of the same message that he routinely delivered to Japan while Treasury Secretary in the Clinton administration, urging the Japanese government to take steps to fix the deep regulatory and financial problems and to urgently deal with its desperate bad loan situation. \par \par \uc2  "The banking system will need to liquidate those loans and operate on a true credit basis going forward," he said, adding that he realized such a policy would create further short-term pain for Japan, already teetering on the brink of recession. \par \par \uc2  "The change necessary to get back on a healthy, long-term track will almost certainly create short-term problems," Rubin said. \par \par \uc2  Asked what Japan could do to stimulate its economy using monetary policy, Rubin declined to comment, professing not to know much about the subject. But he did jokingly make a dig at Bank of Japan Gov. Masaru Hayami, noting that Hayami recently said he was concerned about hyper-inflation in Japan, a country caught in a deflationary spiral. "When the Governor of the Bank of Japan said he was concerned about hyper-inflation, I don't think that's the mindset I would have," he said jokingly. "But then again, I'm not an expert on monetary policy." \par \par \uc2  -By Grainne McCarthy; Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2381; Grainne.McCarthy@Dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (This article was originally published Wednesday) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--A weaker yen won't solve Japan's deep economic problems, former U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin said Wednesday, adding that advocating a weak yen as the solution to Japan's malaise could actually do more harm than good. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think a weak yen is the answer to Japan's economic problems," Rubin told a gathering hosted by the Japan society and Salomon Smith Barney (C). "I think the answer to Japan's economic problems lies in the area of structural reform, things like open credit markets and dealing with fiscal problems." \par \par \uc2  In the weeks since Sept. 11, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been extremely active in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly to weaken the yen against both the dollar and the euro. In doing so, analysts say the BOJ - acting on the order of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  - sent a clear message that it would prefer to see the dollar above Y120 against the yen. Many Japanese policy makers advocate a weaker yen, arguing that it will give a much needed boost to Japanese exporters and help stimulate the overall economy. \par \par \uc2  Rubin appeared to dispute. "I'm not saying the weak yen might not have a short-term effect ... but it is actually harmful to think of the weak yen as an answer to Japan's problems because it could deter people from facing the issues they need to face, which are the hard issues." \par \par \uc2  Rubin reaffirmed much of the same message that he routinely delivered to Japan while Treasury Secretary in the Clinton administration, urging the Japanese Government to take steps to fix the deep regulatory and financial problems and to urgently deal with its desperate bad loan situation. \par \par \uc2  "The banking system will need to liquidate those loans and operate on a true credit basis going forward," he said, adding that he realized such a policy would create further short-term pain for Japan, already teetering on the brink of recession. \par \par \uc2  "The change necessary to get back on a healthy, long-term track will almost certainly create short-term problems," Rubin said. \par \par \uc2  Asked what Japan could do to stimulate its economy using monetary policy, Rubin declined to comment, professing not to know much about the subject. But he did jokingly make a dig at Bank of Japan Gov. Masaru Hayami, noting that Hayami recently said he was concerned about hyper-inflation in Japan, a country caught in a deflationary spiral. "When the Governor of the Bank of Japan said he was concerned about hyper-inflation, I don't think that's the mindset I would have," he said jokingly. "But then again, I'm not an expert on monetary policy." \par \par \uc2  -Grainne McCarthy; Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2381; Grainne.McCarthy@Dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020011018dxai000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
