\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro regains ground vs yen, pressured by dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sumeet Desai \par \uc2 969 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Oktober 2001\par 14:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Oct 18 (Reuters) - The euro regained ground against the yen on Thursday but came under gentle pressure from the dollar, dipping to its lowest level this week on expectations the worst of the economic data after September 11 might be past. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's relatively upbeat assessment of U.S. economic prospects on Wednesday had raised expectations the worst of the bad data may be over. They were waiting for a key report from the Philadelphia Fed later in the day. \par \par \uc2  But traders said activity remained thin and noted a general reluctance to take new positions while uncertainty about the global environment continued to dominate. \par \par \uc2  "The anthrax story is becoming a little tired and we're seeing people more willing to hold dollars. But people aren;t willing to take large positions," said Michael Metcalfe, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Credit Agricole Indosuez. \par \par \uc2  "We've had all the September (U.S. numbers) and they've all been bad. There's some feeling the data could bounce back in October." \par \par \uc2  U.S. weekly jobless claims rose to 490,000 in the October 13 week. \par \par \uc2  At 1224 GMT, the dollar was trading at $0.9016 after briefly dipping to around $0.90 at one point, its lowest level this week. Meanwhile, the one dollar fetched around 121 yen while one euro got 109.14 yen. \par \par \uc2  "The euro has bounced against the yen but it looks purely technically driven," said Ian Stannard, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas. \par \par \uc2  "Yesterday the euro declined sharply. From 109.30 yen it got down to 108.95. We've seen a bounce from there breaking back above 109.30. But we need to watch the sustainability of the move," said Stannard. \par \par \uc2  EICHEL CUTS GROWTH FORECAST \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the longer term outlook for the euro had hardly been helped by a cut in Germany's growth forecast by Finance Minister Hans Eichel early in the session. \par \par \uc2  He said Germany's economy was likely to grow by around 0.75 percent this year and by between 1.0 and 1.5 percent in 2002. The government's official forecast is two percent but officials have been saying for months that growth will be lower. \par \par \uc2  "It's becoming clearer that the euro zone is going to see very slow growth this year. The market has been very reluctant to buy the euro," said Jane Foley, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist, at Barclays Capital. \par \par \uc2  Attention was now focused on the German Ifo Institute's business sentiment report due on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts noted that any signs German business confidence was hit hard by the Sept 11 attacks on the U.S. could knock the euro. \par \par \uc2  They pointed out that Greenspan had noted that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices had been relatively unaffected by the Sept 11 attacks because "we're all being impacted equally." \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in its October monthly bulletin on Thursday that the Sept 11 attacks could delay a rebound in the euro zone's sluggish economy, but repeated that its monetary stance was appropriate at the moment. \par \par \uc2  Echoing a statement from last Thursday, when it kept its interest rates on hold, the bank said that it would very closely monitor economic developments given the high state of global uncertainty. \par \par \uc2  It said that even without the impact of the attacks, the euro zone economy would not have rebounded quickly. \par \par \uc2  "The euro's bound to have reacted to all the bad news from the U.S. and the Middle East but when we focus back on Europe it is going to come back under pressure. We had Eichel this morning for example," said Stannard. \par \par \uc2  VOICE FROM THE PAST \par \par \uc2  Comments from former U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin, which had nudged the dollar back down against the yen, had largely lost significance for the markets by mid-morning trade. \par \par \uc2  Rubin said on Wednesday that structural reform, rather than a weaker yen, was the key to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic problems. \par \par \uc2  Because \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly to pull the dollar up since September 11, his remarks were seen by some in the market as reducing the likelihood of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The dollar, therefore, weakened. \par \par \uc2  Analysts in London said the market had simply got this wrong, pointing out that Rubin was now chairman of Citigroup's executive committee and no longer held a policy-making position. \par \par \uc2  "Rubin is probably right in what he says about the Japanese economy. But he is not calling the shots on Bank of Japan activity in the market. So, why should we really care what he says," said one trader. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 1228 GMT.  All data taken from \par \par \uc2  Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close \par \par \uc2  at 2130 GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                        Last     US Close  % Chg   YTD %   2000\par                                 17 Oct                    Close\par                        -----------------------------------------\par  Euro/dlr              0.9025   0.9035   -0.11    -4.21   0.9422\par  Dlr/yen               120.90   121.23   -0.27    +5.80   114.27\par  Eur