\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro up vs yen but wariness keeps trade hesitant. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Robin Shepherd \par \uc2 614 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Oktober 2001\par 12:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Oct 18 (Reuters) - The euro firmed a quarter of a percent versus the yen on Thursday as a general reluctance to trade in an environment of global uncertainty left dealers relying on technical chart levels to help the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rebound. \par \par \uc2  The dollar and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed little movement as analysts paused to consider the latest anthrax scares being reported from as far afield as Kenya and Australia. \par \par \uc2  "The euro has bounced against the yen but it looks purely technically driven," said Ian Stannard, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas. \par \par \uc2  The euro was quoted at 109.18 against the yen at 1025 GMT. \par \par \uc2  "Yesterday the euro declined sharply. From 109.30 yen it got down to 108.95. We've seen a bounce from there breaking back above 109.30. But we need to watch the sustainability of the move," said Stannard. \par \par \uc2  The dollar last traded at $0.9016 to the euro and at 121.08 yen, barely changed from its New York close. \par \par \uc2  EICHEL CUTS GROWTH FORECAST \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the longer term outlook for the euro had hardly been helped by a cut in Germany's growth forecast by Finance Minister Hans Eichel early in the session. \par \par \uc2  He said Germany's economy was likely to grow by around 0.75 percent this year and by between 1.0 and 1.5 percent in 2002. The government's official forecast is two percent but officials have been saying for months that growth will be lower. \par \par \uc2  "It's becoming clearer that the euro zone is going to see very slow growth this year. The market has been very reluctant to buy the euro," said Jane Foley, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist, at Barclays Capital. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in its October monthly bulletin on Thursday that the September 11 attacks could delay a rebound in the euro zone's sluggish economy, but repeated that its monetary stance was appropriate at the moment. \par \par \uc2  Echoing a statement from last Thursday, when it kept its interest rates on hold, the bank said that it would very closely monitor economic developments given the high state of global uncertainty. \par \par \uc2  It said that even without the impact of the attacks, the euro zone economy would not have rebounded quickly. \par \par \uc2  "The euro's bound to have reacted to all the bad news from the U.S. and the Middle East but when we focus back on Europe it is going to come back under pressure. We had Eichel this morning for example," said Stannard. \par \par \uc2  VOICE FROM THE PAST \par \par \uc2  Comments from former U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin, which had nudged the dollar back down against the yen, had largely lost significance for the markets by mid-morning trade. \par \par \uc2  Rubin said on Wednesday that structural reform, rather than a weaker yen, was the key to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic problems. \par \par \uc2  Because \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly to pull the dollar up since September 11, his remarks were seen by some in the market as reducing the likelihood of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The dollar, therefore, weakened. \par \par \uc2  Analysts in London said the market had simply got this wrong, pointing out that Rubin was now chairman of Citigroup's executive committee and no longer held a policy-making position. \par \par \uc2  "Rubin is probably right in what he says about the Japanese economy. But he is not calling the shots on Bank of Japan activity in the market. So, why should we really care what he says," said one trader. \par \par \uc2  Later in the session, markets will watch for U.S. weekly jobless claims due at 1230 GMT and the Philadelphia Fed survey of manufacturing activity at 1600 GMT. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011018dxai04k23\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
