\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar gains as growth outlooks depress euro, yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Charlotte Cooper \par \uc2 701 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Oktober 2001\par 10:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Oct 16 (Reuters) - The dollar gained a third of a percent against the euro on Tuesday as perceptions grew that, in contrast to the United States, European authorities were doing little to boost growth. \par \par \uc2  Concern over Japan's lack of progress in turning its economy around also depressed the yen which fell to within half a percent of its lowest levels against the dollar since September 11. While uncertainty continues over the impact on U.S. growth from the recent spate of anthrax bacteria cases and what this could do to consumer confidence, analysts and traders said in the short term this was outweighed by the U.S. administration's efforts to boost the economy longer term. \par \par \uc2  "There is a growing realisation that Japan is backed into a corner in terms of how it can kick-start its economy," said a trader at a Japanese bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "People are thinking it's probably better to be long of dollars." \par \par \uc2  In Europe, euro zone finance ministers meeting on Monday called for more interest rate cuts to help prevent recession in the region but European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials made plain their reluctance to ease too quickly or see spending rise too much. \par \par \uc2  "This highlights to the market that although there are very gloomy months ahead for the U.S., we're in for equally gloomy months ahead for the euro zone economy as well," said Derek Halpenny, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi in London. \par \par \uc2  "Although at the end of next year we'll probably look back and Europe will have done better than the U.S., we're still tinkering on negative growth here in Europe also and there's no demand for euros on the back of that," he said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was up more than a third of percent against the euro at $0.9052 although it was still in familiar ranges. Traders said a break of the psychological $0.90 level would darken the euro's chart outlook and send it back to key support at around $0.8950. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the dollar was a third of a percent higher at 121.20. \par \par \uc2  JAPAN'S STRUCTURAL PROBLEMS \par \par \uc2  Bank of Japan governor Masaru Hayami said it was inappropriate for Japan to set a target on providing liquidity and that monetary easing would have little impact while Japan's structural problems remained. \par \par \uc2  Hayami also said he hoped a plan to revamp the state-funded Resolution and Collection Corp to help banks dispose of their bad loans would be quickly passed into law. \par \par \uc2  Traders said this was key to government structural reforms to resolve Japan's long-standing bad loan problem but scepticism reigned over how effective this would be. \par \par \uc2  The government also said on Tuesday it would compile a two trillion yen supplementary budget this month to boost the economy and implement Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi's structural reforms. It also said it would continue to try to cap new bond issuance below the target of 30 trillion yen. \par \par \uc2  But as the market expected any dollar weakness to be met by Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , this was keeping the dolalr in a tight range. \par \par \uc2  "People feel it's risky to keep selling the dollar, even if they're not ready to buy the dollar," said Hideyuki Tsukamoto, a manager at Fuji Bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  department. \par \par \uc2  EUROZONE BICKERING \par \par \uc2  ECB monetary policy was again under scrutiny, with several euro zone finance ministers hinting more could be done to boost growth. Belgian Finance Minister Didier Reynders said on Monday Europe has more scope to stimulate growth via interest rates than via fiscal policy. \par \par \uc2  "With annual inflation falling we should really at this stage be seeing more aggressive monetary action from the ECB but unfortunately going forward from here they're going to continue to be reluctant," Halpenny said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the lack of action in Europe contrasted sharply with U.S. efforts to boost growth and the potential for outlook for recovery later in 2002. \par \par \uc2  Dealers were looking to Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's testimony before Congress on Wednesday for clues on the U.S. economy,. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011016dxag00i7l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
