\par\par \b \uc2  Japan Coalition Noda: No Need For Y30T JGB Cap This FY \b0\par\par\uc2 380 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Oktober 2001\par 07:12\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The Japanese government will likely drop its pledge to cap new government bond issuance at Y30 trillion in the current fiscal year, a senior member of the ruling coalition said Monday. \par \par \uc2  "I strongly told Prime Minister (Junichiro Koizumi) that Japan doesn't need to stick to the Y30 trillion limit," Takeshi Noda, leader of the Conservative Party, said. \par \par \uc2  "I think things will go in that direction," he added. \par \par \uc2  The economy is currently in a far worse state than that foreseen when the Y30 trillion cap was put forward, Noda said. \par \par \uc2  "Nominal gross domestic product for this fiscal year could be worse than minus 4.0%, and there is no way it will be a positive figure," he added. Noda's comments suggest the JGB limit set by reformist Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi will be difficult to meet considering Japan's economic weakness combined with the expected sharp decline in tax revenues. \par \par \uc2  The Conservative Party head also said more public funds may need to be injected into banks. \par \par \uc2  "Banks need to revive their (cash) lending \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . I think public funds should be injected if necessary," he said. \par \par \uc2  Moreover, Noda said a demand-boosting plan is also needed to help the banking sector, thereby lifting the whole economy. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "A plan to boost demand is a priority issue, rather than keeping the\par cap on new bond issuance at Y30 trillion," Noda added.\par - - 15/10/01 06-32G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Noda also said a strong dollar is in the interest of both the United\par States and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , suggesting the government will look for monetary\par authorities to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market if the dollar drops too far.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The Bank of Japan has been active (in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market,)\par to keep the dollar supported. A strong dollar is in the interest of both\par Japan and the U.S.," Noda said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Having said that, the BOJ can't decide to take action on its own," he\par added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The BOJ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's agent last month and earlier this month to buoy the U.S.\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after the Sept. 11 terrorist attacks.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Yukiko Tsuba, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par yukiko.tsuba@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011015dxaf0008s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
