\par\par \b \uc2  Shiokawa: Will Take  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Steps If Necessary \b0\par\par\uc2 432 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2001\par 05:52\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  (Corrected at 0354GMT) \par \par \uc2  0352GMT \par \par \uc2  - - 12/10/01 03-54G \par \par \uc2  0354GMT \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar has recovered from levels hit after the Sept. 11 terrorist attacks against the yen, but this situation may change if the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  lets its guard down, Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said Friday. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates have returned to their levels against the dollar seen before the terrorist attacks, but this may change if we let our guard down," Shiokawa said in Parliament committee testimony. \par \par \uc2  "Exchange rates should reflect fundamentals and we have to correctly predict when large fluctuations in rates will occur and take appropriate steps as necessary," he added. \par \par \uc2  MOF directed the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to buy dollars and euros for yen in the weeks following the terrorist attacks in order to prevent a rapid rise by the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   -Tokyo Bureau, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929\par - - 12/10/01 04-39G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0439GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said Friday he told Prime Minister\par Junichiro Koizumi he would like to see the yen weaken slightly from its\par current levels.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We talked about \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and stocks and I explained the\par dollar is trading at about 120 to 121 yen. And I also said I don't have\par a very strong desire one way or the other, but I would like to see the\par yen a little weaker," Shiokawa said in Parliament committee testimony.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Shiokawa was explaining to other lawmakers in committee what he spoke\par about in a meeting with Koizumi, Chief Cabinet Secretary Yasuo Fukuda,\par and Economy Minister Heizo Takenaka Thursday evening.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Shiokawa also said the Finance Ministry will probably be able to\par announce next week a revised estimate for tax revenue for the current\par fiscal year, Kyodo News reported.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The ministry is now revising the estimate by incorporating into\par calculations the recent accelerated downturn in the economy as well as\par falling profits at Japanese corporations. The government's current\par estimate stands at Y50.7 trillion for the current year. But it is widely\par expected the new figure will fall more than a trillion yen short, making\par it difficult for Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi to keep the promise of\par limiting the new bond issues to Y30 trillion this fiscal year.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The finance minister said that he hopes to see the consumer prices\par rise to levels seen in 1997.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The price levels have fallen by 1.3% to 1.4% since 1997. I hope they\par will rise back to the 1997 levels," Shiokawa told the parliament.\par -0- 12/10/01 05-36G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011012dxac00079\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
