\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Fears Keeps Yen Trading Lively In Thin Mkt \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Hardy \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 848 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Oktober 2001\par 20:31\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Clashes between \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders' attempts to establish long-yen positions and the determination of Japanese authorities to avoid a stronger yen have provided action to an otherwise lethargic \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  U.S.-led airstrikes against Afghanistan and the potential for a long-drawn land-based military campaign against the country&#160;s Taliban rulers have kept market participants cautious. Since Sept. 11, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading has generally been lackluster, except in the dollar/yen and euro/yen pairs. \par \par \uc2  Traders report that the dollar was trading quietly against both the euro and the yen in late London activity Wednesday when a 20-minute selling spurt pushed dollar/yen down 60 basis-points to its low for the day at Y119.80. \par \par \uc2  While the yen buying was dramatic, it ended quickly as traders backed off for fear of renewed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollar/yen by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Since Sept. 17, the BOJ has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  seven times in markets and bought close to $30 billion to slow the yen's appreciation. While the latest fall in dollar/yen ran out of steam before bringing about new \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders were convinced that further selling would have been met with BOJ action and those holding speculative long-yen positions could have been hit hard. \par \par \uc2  One dealer suggested that further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be seen as early as "the low Y119s." \par \par \uc2  In later trading, the ensuing scramble to adjust positions led to a solid rebound for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In early afternoon trading in New York, the dollar was at Y120.33, down from Y120.47 in London but above Y120.20 late Tuesday. The euro was at $0.9118, down slightly from $0.9120 in London and below $0.9127 late Tuesday and was at Y109.75, little changed from Y109.76 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Overnight, Japanese authorities again left no doubt about their strategy to discourage further yen buying. \par \par \uc2  Zembei Mizoguchi, head of the International Bureau at the Finance Ministry, reiterated that the Japanese government is maintaining its stance on opposing an excessive rise in the yen against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Our wish to curb excessive yen strength is unchanged," Mizoguchi told a group of reporters on his arrival at the ministry. \par \par \uc2  A stronger yen makes Japanese exports more expensive and therefore less attractive in foreign markets. Because Japan is heavily export-oriented, a drop in exports could delay efforts to end the current economic slump. \par \par \uc2  Mizoguchi also stated that the finance ministers of the Group of Seven highly industrialized countries had agreed at their meeting in Washington D.C. last Saturday to cooperate in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as necessary. \par \par \uc2  Larry Greenberg, international economist at Ried, Thunberg in Westport Conn., thinks the Japanese authorities are making too much of the strength of the yen and may have to recognize that it could make further gains. \par \par \uc2  "In contrast to all the fuss made by Japanese officials about the yen not reflecting fundamentals and needing to be weaker, dollar/yen lies quite near to its 14-year and 5-year moving averages of Y120.40 and Y118.40," he said in a client research note. \par \par \uc2  But given the prevailing Japanese view of current dollar/yen levels, Greenberg suggests "\b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have to resume." \par \par \uc2  In the eyes of traders, the dollar/yen pair is delicately poised at current levels. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "As long as we hold above Y120 and thereabouts, there probably won't\par be any need for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said Patrick Brodie, assistant vice\par president for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at Sumitomo Mitsui Banking Corporation in\par New York, "but they don't want it to fall below Y115."\par    Like many others, Brodie expects BOJ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to resume not far\par below Wednesday's levels saying,   "At Y118, they would probably start\par getting active again."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par However, analysts point out that part of the reason for the Japanese\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's recent strength is a reaction to the disappointing\par performance of the euro.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par As has often been seen in the past, the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has failed to\par take advantage of problems besetting the dollar and this lack of\par positive momentum has prompted the closure of long-euro positions\par against the yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Despite Japan's chronic economic problems, euro/yen - which many once\par considered a solid long-term buy - topped out earlier this month around\par Y111.77 and has since slipped back below Y110.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In a client note Wednesday, Bob Sinche, head of global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par strategy at Citibank in New York, leaves no doubt of his position in\par favor of the yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "In our view, risks of euro/yen selling continues," he wrote.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Sinche then argues that the BOJ may not act  aggressively enough to\par boost liquidity in the Japanese economy and concludes: "lack of BOJ\par action could lead the markets to review large yen short positions and\par could contribute to a test of the MOF/BOJ resolve to hold dollar/yen\par above the Y120 level"\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -John Hardy, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2122; john.hardy@dowjones\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011010dxaa00k22\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
