\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar inches up but capped by uncertainty. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Isabel Reynolds \par \uc2 704 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Oktober 2001\par 04:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Oct 9 (Reuters) - The dollar firmed slightly against the yen on Tuesday morning, little daunted by the United States' second round of nighttime air strikes against Afghanistan overnight. \par \par \uc2  The greenback pushed up over the psychologically important 120.00 yen barrier in mid-morning, but lacked the strength to rise further in thin trade after a three-day weekend in Japan and the United States. \par \par \uc2  By late morning it was at 119.98 yen , up from 119.83 in London late on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "There is no inclination to buy the dollar up from 120 yen," a dealer at a Japanese city bank said. \par \par \uc2  "Despite fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the uncertainty means there is an increasing risk of the dollar's falling," he added. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the launch of retaliatory assaults by U.S. and British forces had been anticipated for so long that they barely weakened the dollar. \par \par \uc2  But many players were avoiding taking new positions, as they see the possibility of revenge attacks on U.S. soil as very strong. A second case of anthrax diagnosed in Florida at the weekend was seen as adding to the generally nervous atmosphere. \par \par \uc2  Anthrax is a rare disease that experts say can be used as a biological warfare agent. \par \par \uc2  Market players were also eager to see how U.S. equities would perform later on Tuesday. Stocks slipped only slightly on Monday after the first round of attacks, but trade was extremely thin due to the Columbus Day holiday. \par \par \uc2  ALONE ON \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  "It remains difficult for the dollar to rise unless U.S. equities rise, so people are looking to the euro and temporarily to the Swiss franc," said Koji Fukaya, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  The euro slipped against the dollar to stand around 91.97 cents in late morning, compared with 92.10 cents in early trade. It held out against the yen, bid at 110.37 yen in late morning. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also has only limited room to fall, with the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  still looming at the lower ends. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said Japan had not made a decision yet as to whether further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was needed after the attacks, but added there may be a need if speculative moves destabilise the market. \par \par \uc2  The meeting of Group of Seven Finance Ministers and central bankers had agreed at their weekend meeting that decisions on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be left up to individual countries, he added. \par \par \uc2  "There will be no coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ... But we are seeing a situation where there is a chance for speculative moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Countries need to watch their own markets and make their own efforts for stability," Shiokawa said. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  seven days last month in an effort to protect Japanese exporters by preventing the yen from soaring out of control. \par \par \uc2  "At least this means he has effectively got authorisation from other countries for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so that will help keep the dollar firm," said Tokyo-Mitsubishi's Fukaya. \par \par 