\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar on defensive as U.S. goes on attack. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christina Fincher \par \uc2 595 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2001\par 14:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Oct 8 (Reuters) - The dollar fell on Monday, losing as much as one percent against the Swiss franc as nervous investors sought safe-havens after U.S.-led strikes on Afghanistan. \par \par \uc2  The greenback also shed over half a percent against the yen and a third of a percent against the euro. But it rose against sterling as Britain's participation in the Afghanistan strikes, as well as heavy corporate selling, made the pound the worst performer of the pack. \par \par \uc2  The strikes - the first military response to the September 11 attacks on the United States - were not unexpected, but dealers said a lack of clarity over the duration of the conflict and a cautious response from several Muslim countries had compounded market jitters. \par \par \uc2  "Uncertainty is breeding nervousness and the typical knee-jerk reaction has been to sell dollars and buy Swiss francs," said Mark Henry, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at GNI. \par \par \uc2  "A fairly critical response to the strikes from several countries means the situation may be more difficult for the United States to sort out than some people thought." \par \par \uc2  The dollar's fall to a one-week low below the psychological 120 yen barrier put dealers on their guard for further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is concerned the yen's strength may harm its fragile economy and ordered its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on seven days last month, mostly to sell yen and buy dollars. Wariness of further action was helping to stem the dollar's losses, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  SWISS FRANC GAINS \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc was the biggest gainer of the session, appreciating close to 1.60 to the dollar for the first time in almost two weeks. \par \par \uc2  "The Swiss franc is benefiting from renewed risk aversion because there is no obvious end in sight to the conflict," said Shahab Jalinoos, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS Warburg. \par \par \uc2  "There is still plenty of resistance in the Islamic world to the strikes and support for the United States may not be as strong as it was during the Gulf crisis." \par \par \uc2  Swizerland's political neutrality and its huge current account surplus have made it a magnet for investors fleeing riskier assets. \par \par \uc2  At 13 percent of gross domestic product, Switzerland's current account surplus is among the biggest in the world. In contrast, the U.S. runs a hefty current account deficit and needs to attract over $1 billion a day in foreign capital to offset downward pressure on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc was the best performing major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the aftermath of the September 11 attacks, surging to 20-month peaks against the dollar and all-time highs against the euro. Since then, aggressive cuts in Swiss interest rates have spurred sone unwinding of these gains. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc's gains against the dollar also helped the euro to the day's highs above $0.92. But the euro was down slightly against the yen at 110.30 yen. \par \par \uc2  STERLING DRAGGED DOWN \par \par \uc2  Sterling was the worst performing major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , shedding more than one percent against the euro and half a percent against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was being hurt both by Britain's involvement in the strikes on Afganistan and by talk of merger and acquisition-related outflows. A British bank was said to be selling around two billion euros worth of sterling in the early session. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said price movements were exaggerated by thin trading volumes, with a market holiday in Japan and a partial market holiday in the United States. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011008dxa800nb4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
