\par\par \b \uc2  Japan Gets G7 Nod Of Approval For  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Steps; No More \b0\par\par\uc2  By Arran Scott \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 988 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2001\par 09:11\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON -(Dow Jones)- Japan got a nod of approval from the Group of Seven for its single-handed efforts to tame the rampaging yen, but little more. \par \par \uc2  Wrapping up a meeting Saturday, G7 finance ministers and central bankers agreed the Sept. 11 terrorist attacks would dent the global economy, and pledged to take steps to stimulate growth and keep financial markets healthy. \par \par \uc2  Japan, which fought furiously in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to curb the yen's surge after the attacks precipitated a plunge in the dollar, secured the "understanding" of the G7 for its aggressive yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month, senior Japanese finance ministry officials said. \par \par \uc2  But \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's G7 partners offered little more. \par \par \uc2  "We will continue to monitor exchange markets closely and cooperate as appropriate," the G7 said in a statement - essentially the same wording as the last statement issued in April. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa told a news conference after the meeting that the G7 didn't discuss coordinated action to weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  That was generally expected, as Shiokawa had basically taken the issue off the table earlier this week, when he said he didn't plan to seek help from his G7 partners - Canada, the U.S., Germany, the U.K., France, and Italy. \par \par \uc2  Japan was widely expected to seek at least implicit approval from its peers regarding its recent action in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. In that sense, by securing G7 "understanding" Japan likely accomplished a key objective in Washington. \par \par \uc2  Still, Japanese officials will leave Washington with little more than what they came with. And though the G7 agreed to cooperate as necessary in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, Japan will likely have to act on its own if the yen takes off again. \par \par \uc2  Curbing yen strength is a make-or-break game for Japan. With the deflation-mired economy already in a steep downturn as exports slump, a sharp rise in the yen could depress exports even more and accelerate price declines. \par \par \uc2  In a series of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month estimated to have topped $20 billion, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  jacked the dollar back above the key Y120 level after its plunge to a low of Y115.80 in the wake of the attacks. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say Japan can continue to put a lid on the yen if it wishes, because monetary authorities there can sell an infinite amount of their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , if push comes to shove. \par \par \uc2  "How the market responds is up to the market," a senior Japanese finance ministry official said, commenting on the G7's statement. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Japan Eyes Deregulating Econ, Cleaning Up Bank Sector\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Despite heightened near-term uncertainties stemming from the terrorist\par attacks, G7 officials agreed that longer-term fundamentals are sound and\par were upbeat about future prospects.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We are strongly committed to bringing forward needed measures to\par increase economic growth and preserve the health of our financial\par markets," the G7 statement read in part.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par For its part, Japan told its G7 partners it will press ahead with\par structural reforms, deregulating the economy and taking steps to clear\par away bad loans clogging up its financial system.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Shiokawa explained Tokyo's plans to bolster a government-backed entity\par to help banks clean up their balance sheets - a move that foreign\par observers have long urged Tokyo to consider.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In its latest effort to reinvigorate the ailing economy, the Japanese\par government outlined last month plans to require banks to adopt more\par stringent assessments of their loan portfolios, and to expand the role\par of the Resolution and Collection Corp. to buy sour loans from financial\par institutions.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Analysts generally applauded the thrust of the plan, but criticized\par the government for not providing enough details.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Despite the bumps ahead for Japan's economy, Shiokawa also said Tokyo\par remains committed to fiscal reform, symbolized by Prime Minister\par Junichiro Koizumi's plan to cap annual issuance of government bonds at\par Y30 trillion.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The Y30 trillion (bond cap) will be carried out as we have planned,"\par Shiokawa told reporters.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In line with Koizumi's stance to take "bold and flexible" measures in\par the event of sharp economic deterioration, however, Shiokawa left the\par door open just a tad to a possible change in the government's stance.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "If there are serious effects from the terrorist (attacks), I think we\par would take appropriate measures as needed," Shiokawa said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    BOJ Still Under The Gun To Loosen Monetary Policy\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Bank of Japan flooded the market with funds in the wake of the\par terrorist attacks, in step with the world's most powerful central banks,\par to prevent disruptions in settlements and to calm markets.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Easing monetary policy Sept. 18, the BOJ adopted a target of "Y6\par trillion or more" for current-account reserves, and subsequently pumped\par nearly Y13 trillion into the banking system - more than doubling its\par quantitative easing from pre-attack amounts.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The BOJ has since drained much of the excess funds from the market, a\par move that prompted Shiokawa last week to call on the bank to maintain at\par least Y8 trillion in current-account reserves.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Shiokawa backtracked on that remark Saturday, saying he hadn't made\par any demands of the BOJ, and mentioned the Y8 trillion figure because\par that was the level when the BOJ eased Sept. 18.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami told reporters after the G7 meeting that\par the BOJ would continue to provide ample funds to the market, keeping an\par eye on the market situation.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Still, the BOJ, which has long come under pressure to do more to fight\par deflation, remains under the gun.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Horst Koehler, managing director of the International Monetary Fund,\par called on the BOJ Friday to loosen credit further.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "In Japan, there is still scope for more decisive monetary action,"\par Koehler said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Hayami said no G7 official echoed Koehler's remarks at the meeting\par Saturday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Arran Scott, Dow Jones Newswires; 240-888-0753;\par arran.scott@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011007dxa70002o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
