\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-G7 did not discuss joint  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2 -Shiokawa. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoko Nishikawa \par \uc2 790 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2001\par 07:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, Oct 6 (Reuters) - Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said on Saturday that while the Group of Seven economic powers agreed on appropriate cooperation on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, they stopped short of talking about jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "As you see in the (G7) statement, we said we would continue to monitor exchange markets closely and cooperate as appropriate," Shiokawa told a news conference after the meeting of G7 finance ministers and central bankers in Washington. \par \par \uc2  But he added: "We did not talk about concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on seven days since Sept. 17, mostly selling yen and buying dollars to try to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The yen strengthened to a seven-month high of around 115.87 yen per dollar in the aftermath of the September 11 attacks on the United States. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was standing above 120 yen on Saturday. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo fears that an excessive rise in the yen could harm the nation's fragile and export-driven economy, which is on the verge of slipping into its fourth recession in a decade. \par \par \uc2  The G7 finance ministers' words in their communique - "We will continue to monitor exchange markets closely and cooperate as appropriate" - closely reflected those they have made about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in their past communiques. \par \par \uc2  A senior Japanese finance ministry official said G7 colleagues expressed understanding on Tokyo's stance at the meeting - though they did not go into details - when Shiokawa explained about Japan's recent action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  On some occasions, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought euros for yen on Japan's behalf. Once in the past the New York Federal Reserve has sold yen for Japan as well, but all of those \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  were funded by Japan. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa has stressed that Tokyo remained ready to take appropriate action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on its own if necessary, but some market participants remained sceptical of whether solo action by Japan could keep preventing the yen from rising again. \par \par \uc2  REFORMS AND JGB CAP \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa said Japan would like to contribute to the world economic recovery by improving the Japanese economy. \par \par \uc2  At the meeting, Shiokawa explained to his G7 counterparts about Japan's plan to push forward reforms, including deregulation, promotion of banks' bad loan disposal and the government's timetable for reform plans. \par \par \uc2  Unlike recent G7 statements, Saturday's did not have separate sections on member countries' economic conditions, including Japan. It was shortened to have a greater impact as a message to markets and the world, the Japanese official said. \par \par \uc2  "Each country has different economic conditions," the official said, adding that the G7 agreed that it was up to each nation to decide what kind of steps to take, instead of coming up with coordinated measures, to increase global economic growth. \par \par \uc2  "For Japan, as the Finance Minister says, promoting structural reforms is the most important," he added. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa said the government would keep new Japanese government bond (JGB) issuance below 30 trillion yen per year as promised regardless of possible economic impact from the September 11 attacks on the United States. \par \par \uc2  "We have not reached a situation where we lack funds to keep the 30 trillion yen cap after the terrorist attacks," he said. \par \par \uc2  The government of Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi has pledged to keep new JGB to 30 trillion yen a year in a bid to rein in public debt, which is approaching 130 percent of GDP - the worst level among major industrial countries. \par \par \uc2  But speculation is rife that the government would not be able to keep the promise, especially as tax revenues are seen falling short of estimates due to the economic slowdown. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa said he was not considering any further spending steps in the wake of the September 11 attacks now but added: "If there is a serious impact from the terrorist incident, I think we should take appropriate steps." \par \par \uc2  BOJ TO KEEP AMPLE FUNDS \par \par \uc2  Bank of Japan (BOJ) Governor Masaru Hayami, who also attended the G7 meeting, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue to tackle the problem of deflation in Japan and provide ample liquidity to financial markets. \par \par \uc2  Fund demand had risen after the September 11 attacks and ahead of Japanese companies book-closings at the end of September, prompting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to provide ample liquidity to markets, Hayami said, adding that the situation has stabilised somewhat since then. \par \par \uc2  The International Monetary Fund said on Friday that leading nations should cut interest rates further if needed and that there was "scope for more decisive monetary action" in Japan. \par \par \uc2  But Hayami said there was no talk about whether the BOJ should further ease its already super-loose monetary policy at the G7 meeting. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011007dxa7002mh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
