\par\par \b \uc2  G7 did not discuss joint  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2 -Shiokawa. \b0\par\par\uc2 167 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2001\par 02:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, Oct 6 (Reuters) - Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said on Saturday the Group of Seven finance ministers and central bankers did not discuss joint \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at their meeting. \par \par \uc2  "As you see in the (G7) statement, we said we would continue to monitor exchange markets closely and cooperate as appropriate. We did not talk about concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market)," Shiokawa told a news conference after the meeting. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on seven days since Sept. 17, mostly selling yen and buying dollars in an effort to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which had strengthened to a seven-month high of around 115.87 yen per dollar in the aftermath of the Sept. 11 attacks on the United States. \par \par \uc2  On some occasions, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought euros for the yen on behalf of Japan. Japan once used the New York Federal Reserve to sell the yen as well, but all of those \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  were funded by Japan. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011007dxa700003\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
