\par\par \b \uc2  O'Neill sees US economy pickup mid'02-Japan source. \b0\par\par\uc2 540 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2001\par 22:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, Oct 6 (Reuters) - U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill told his Japanese counterpart in a bilateral meeting that the U.S. economy is likely to pick up around mid-2002, a Japanese finance ministry official said on Saturday. \par \par \uc2  The official added that O'Neill discussed the state of the U.S. economy with Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa, and talked about recent U.S. monetary policy actions and current fiscal proposals to boost the ailing U.S. economy following the Sept. 11 attacks on Washington and New York. \par \par \uc2  "He said the U.S. economy would probably pick up in a reasonable period, around mid next year," the official told reporters on condition of anonymity. \par \par \uc2  "They agreed that sustainable growth in the two biggest economies of the world and maintaining confidence in markets are important for the global economy," the official said. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa explained Tokyo's recent efforts in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to halt the yen's rise, which it fears could hurt its fragile and export-led economy, and O'Neill expressed an understanding of Japan's stance, the official added. \par \par \uc2  Separately, Treasury spokeswoman Michele Davis confirmed that Shiokawa explained the recent actions Japan has taken in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and said, "Secretary O'Neill said he understood Japan's position." \par \par \uc2  The Japanese official, however, said the two ministers did not discuss future \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements and that O'Neill did not discuss U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy at the meeting. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on seven days since September 17, mostly selling yen and buying dollars in an effort to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which had strengthened to a seven-month high of around 115.87 yen per dollar in the aftermath of the attacks. \par \par \uc2  On some occasions, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought euros for the yen on behalf of Japan. Japan once used the New York Federal Reserve to sell the yen as well, but all of those \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  were funded by Japan. \par \par \uc2  Asked if Shiokawa asked for U.S. cooperation in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market at the bilateral meeting, the official said, "They did not talk anything more than what I have so far said." \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa met O'Neill ahead of a meeting of finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors from the Group of Seven nations - the United States, Britain, Canada, France, Germany, Italy and Japan. \par \par \uc2  At the bilateral meeting, Shiokawa explained Japan's recent efforts to promote disposal of bad bank loans and reform stock-related taxes, as well as the Bank of Japan's recent provision of ample liquidity to markets. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa expressed to O'Neill the resolve of Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi's administration to press ahead with reform plans. O'Neill in return welcomed Japan's efforts to push forward reforms and deal with it bad loan problem, the official said. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the day, BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami met with European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg and separately with Bank of England Governor Sir Edward George and exchanged views on economic conditions and monetary policy in the wake of the attacks, a BOJ official said. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, Hayami discussed similar topics at a bilateral meeting with U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011006dxa600q8k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
