\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar dawdles in range ahead of US jobs report. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chisa Fujioka \par \uc2 723 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Oktober 2001\par 08:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Oct 5 (Reuters) - The dollar loitered in a tight range on Friday as market players kept moves to a minimum ahead of the release of a U.S. jobs report later in the day and a weekend meeting of Group of Seven (G7) finance ministers. \par \par \uc2  A three-day weekend ahead in both Japan and the United States also sapped volume, with Japan off on Monday for Health-Sports Day and U.S. offices closed for Columbus Day. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment for the dollar weakened slightly overnight after U.S unemployment claims surged to their highest level in nine years. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar seems to be losing energy to test much higher," said a Japanese bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was also bruised after a Russian airliner carrying up to 78 passengers and crew en route from Tel Aviv to Siberia crashed in what the Russian authorities said may have been a terrorist act. \par \par \uc2  "The Russian airliner news last night was a reminder of the negatives surrounding the dollar these days and in that sense, European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are most likely to benefit," the bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The news, coming after the suicide plane attacks on U.S. cities last month, spooked investors and the dollar was stuck around 120.54/58 yen in late Tokyo from 120.45/55 yen in late U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2  Supporting the dollar was yen-selling against the euro, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  held gains at 110.74/89 yen from 110.57/68 yen in late offshore on some interest from Japanese institutional investors. \par \par \uc2  Against the dollar, it stood at $0.9182/87 from $0.9175/85. \par \par \uc2  PAYROLL, G7 \par \par \uc2  The market took a cautious stance ahead of U.S. jobs data for September due at 1230 GMT, especially after a surge in weekly claims for U.S. unemployment benefits on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The weekly claims climbed to the highest level in nine years, reminding the market that a U.S. recession was a near certainty after last month's attacks. \par \par \uc2  Weekly U.S. jobless claims soared to 528,000 in the week ended September 29. \par \par \uc2  Economist polled by Reuters estimated on average that U.S. payrolls shed 109,000 jobs in September after shrinking by 113,000 jobs in August. They forecast the unemployment rate to rise to 5.0 percent from 4.9 percent. \par \par \uc2  "There is nervousness before the jobs data, but it's not clear whether the upcoming September figures will show the total post-attack impact," said Masayuki Yamamoto, a research analyst at Bank of America's Tokyo branch. \par \par \uc2  Dealers were also watching the outcome of the G7 meeting, although many think \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not be near the top of the agenda. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said on Friday that concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not be a topic of discussion with U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill at their bilateral meeting this weekend. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market late last month to curb an export-damaging rise in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "On \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , we have been acting on our own judgment, not on a coordinated basis," Shiokawa told reporters. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0621 GMT.  All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 \par \par \uc2  GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                        Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2000 Cls\par                                 04 Oct.\par                        -----------------------------------------\par  Euro/dlr              0.9186   0.9178   +0.09    -3.26   0.9496\par  Dlr/yen               120.51   120.44   +0.06    +5.29   114.45\par  Euro/yen              110.69   110.56   +0.12    +1.82   108.71\par  Dlr/swiss             1.6198   1.6199   -0.01    +1.16   1.6012\par  Stg/dlr               1.4763   1.4770   -0.05    -1.58   1.5000\par  Can/dlr               1.5702   1.5689   +0.08    +5.28   1.4914\par  Aus/dlr               0.5014   0.4982   +0.64   -10.37   0.5594\par  Euro/swiss            1.4879   1.4869   +0.07    -2.25   1.5221\par  Euro/stg              0.6220   0.6212   +0.13    -1.66   0.6325.\par \par \par \par     ..        Debt...      Indicators...\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reports..\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par All spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Europe spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Volatilities\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market info from BOJ\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par World \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Official rates...\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Diary.......             Top events........\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Diaries........... Diaries Index........\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Press Digests....         Polls on G7 economies..\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Reuters Television..\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011005dxa500b4a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pi