\par\par \b \uc2  Ex-MOF Kato: G7 Understands Japan Stance Vs Strong Yen \b0\par\par\uc2  By Miyako Takebe \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 1,293 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2001\par 12:04\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The Group of Seven industrial powers understand Japan's need to sell yen in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to halt the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise and provide liquidity to its markets, a former top Finance Ministry official said Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "Given current macroeconomic conditions, the G7 understands \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to put the brakes on a rapid rise in the yen," Takatoshi Kato, former vice finance minister for international affairs, told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview. \par \par \uc2  A sharp rise in the yen could hurt the already-battered economy by making exports more expensive and accelerating domestic price declines. \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities have sold an estimated Y3 trillion to Y4 trillion for dollars and euros in seven \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past two weeks to arrest a steep rise in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the Sept. 11 terrorist attacks on the U.S. \par \par \uc2  "Increasing liquidity is the one of the last policy options left open to Japan as it stands on the brink of a deflationary spiral," Kato said. \par \par \uc2  The G7 "understands that leaving excess funds from yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market is a way for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to provide liquidity to markets," Kato said. \par \par \uc2  The G7 nations are likely to express their understanding when their finance ministers and central bankers gather Saturday in Washington. \par \par \uc2  While they may accept Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy, Kato said, Tokyo's G7 partners - the U.S., Germany, the U.K., France, Canada and Italy - likely won't feel inclined to join in yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Conditions in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are not at the stage where it would be impossible to manage without coordinated G7 action," said Kato, now an adviser to the Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  Kato, who directed Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy when he was vice minister, added that the recent repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have helped "neutralize" the market effects of the terrorist attacks by keeping the dollar from tumbling as investors moved to adjust positions. \par \par \uc2  Weak Japanese economic fundamentals had caused many investors to go long dollars and set up contracts to sell the yen - positions which had to be closed after the attacks gave rise to uncertainty over the U.S. economy and the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped soak up the dollar-selling," and keep markets stable, he said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped buoy the dollar from Y115.80 to above Y120.00, where it was trading before the suicide attacks on the World Trade Center and the Pentagon. \par \par \uc2  Given the subsequent stability in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other markets, Kato said, exchange rates are unlikely to be a major topic at Saturday's G7 meeting. Rather, the ministers and central bankers will likely focus on a coordinated plan to help prevent a global recession. \par \par \uc2  But he added that since it is difficult to determine how the market will move in the near term, it will be necessary to keep a close eye on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Kato said that while fiscal policy - such as the compilation of an extra budget for employment and other economic measures - was necessary for the economy, monetary policy holds the key in pulling Japan out of deflation. \par \par \uc2  "If economic conditions deteriorate further, the Bank of Japan can increase liquidity by increasing its bank-reserves liquidity target and buying more Japanese government bonds outright," said Kato. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The BOJ now floods the market with liquidity to support the economy\par and combat deflation, ramping up its liquidity provision after the Sept.\par 11 attacks. But \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials are cautious about further\par increasing the BOJ's excess-cash target or buying more JGBs to meet it.\par    -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par miyako.takebe@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (This item originally ran at 1004 GMT Thursday)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The Group of Seven industrial powers understand\par Japan's need to sell yen in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to halt the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's rise and provide liquidity to its markets, a former top\par Finance Ministry official said Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Given current macroeconomic conditions, the G7 understands \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's\par need to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to put the brakes on a rapid rise in the yen,"\par Takatoshi Kato, former vice finance minister for international affairs,\par told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f