\par\par \b \uc2  Shiokawa, O'Neill to meet pre-G7 - Japan MOF. \b0\par\par\uc2 215 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2001\par 09:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Oct 4 (Reuters) - U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill and Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa will meet on Saturday to discuss economic issues in the wake of the September 11 attacks, the Japanese Finance Ministry said on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The two - plus their deputies and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs of both countries - will meet shortly before attending a meeting of Group of Seven finance ministers in Washington, Japanese Vice Finance Minister Toshiro Muto told a news conference. "Among the expected topics are the state of the U.S. and Japanese economies and the effect of the terror incident," he said. Asked if \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates or joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be a topic, Muto said \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  could come up during general macroeconomic discussions but that he had not heard if concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be on the agenda. \par \par \uc2  Asked if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  felt it needed to present a fiscal stimulus package of its own to match planned U.S. spending to boost the economy, Muto said: \par \par \uc2  "A long time ago there were times when they would ask for more public works spending, but I haven't heard such voices recently. I think both the United States and Japan share the view that structural reform is crucial for Japan." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011004dxa400fr6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
