\par\par \b \uc2  Euro Strength Against Yen Likely To Have A Limited Life \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nicholas Hastings \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 654 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Oktober 2001\par 16:18\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  LONDON -(Dow Jones)- The euro's climb to a six-month high against the yen Wednesday has put it back in the spotlight as a driving force in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  But analysts warn against getting carried away - either by suggestions that Japanese investors have some new-found interest in the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  or that the euro itself has found a new lease of life. \par \par \uc2  Derek Halpenny, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist with Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi, put the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains down largely to speculative flows as the market's attention remains firmly fixed on the difficulties facing the U.S. "Any euro rally is more default than anything else," he said. \par \par \uc2  Suggestions that the euro/yen pair may have taken on a more directional role in the market emerged as the euro advanced steadily all week to a high of Y111.77 earlier Wednesday, a level last seen back at the end of March. The euro started the week at Y108.50. \par \par \uc2  The move coincided with the start of the second half of the financial year in Japan and talk that this might prompt some reversal of the repatriation flows that helped support the yen up until the end of September. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese names have begun to buy both USD/JPY and EUR/JPY substantially for the first time in weeks while speculation persists that real money managers will begin to reallocate funds for this quarter out of the JPY," according to UBS Warburg's morning adviser from Singapore. \par \par \uc2  This, the bank added, was reflected in minor crosses too, with the pound and the Australian dollar both rising against the yen as well. \par \par \uc2  Others noted that yen flows into the euro represented a diversion from the dollar, which is not only facing the ninth cut in interest rates by the U.S. Federal Reserve this year and continued concern over the state of the U.S. economy, but also the threat of military action against the Taliban which could only be seen as negative. \par \par \uc2  "Institutional investors remain nervous about the global security situation and are increasing their hedge ratios out of concern about what might happen next," reported Marshall Gittler, Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Bank of America in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "They assume that the dollar would fall if the U.S. attacks Afghanistan," he added. \par \par \uc2  But this wasn't the only reason for looking for some flows out of the yen. \par \par \uc2  According to BNP Paribas, portfolio flows have turned negative as foreigners liquidate their Japanese assets in the wake of Monday's highly negative Tankan survey and the continued \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to push the yen lower. \par \par \uc2  In the three weeks since the terrorist attack in the U.S., the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  seven times to prevent the yen from rising too far against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Given all this, BNP Paribas is targeting the Y114 in its trading strategy, suggesting that more gains are on their way. \par \par \uc2  "Pressure on the Bank of Japan will remain massive to loosen monetary conditions further," said Hans Redeker, head of strategy. \par \par \uc2  However, not all are convinced that the current outflow is anything more than short-term players taking a bet rather than some radical adjustment to investment portfolios. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi's Halpenny reckons that with the Japanese government still dragging its feet on corporate and financial restructuring, the sharp rise in risk aversion that took place after Sept. 11 isn't going to go away. \par \par \uc2  As a result, he expects Japanese investors to essentially stay put, keeping their money in Japanese markets. \par \par \uc2  "You are not going to see any fundamental switch in flows in dollar/yen," he said, suggesting that euro's current rise against the yen will soon run out of steam. \par \par \uc2  -By Nicholas Hastings, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 20 7842 9493; nick.hastings@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020011003dxa3005k5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
