\par\par \b \uc2  Ex-BOJ Nakagawa: Dlr At Y115-Y125 "Reasonable" For Japan \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ron Harui \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 801 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Oktober 2001\par 07:43\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The Japanese government wants to weaken the yen to support the nation's flagging economy and fight deflation, a former executive director at the Bank of Japan said Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "There are no tools left to the government, so at the very least it wants to weaken the yen to facilitate exports to help the economy," said Yukitsugu Nakagawa, deputy chairman at the Institute for International Policy Studies. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  seven times in the past two weeks, selling an estimated Y3 trillion for dollars and euros, arguing that the strong yen doesn't reflect fundamentals and could harm the fragile economy. \par \par \uc2  Another objective of the government's current \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is to "stoke inflation" by increasing import prices through a weaker yen, Nakagawa said. \par \par \uc2  By fanning inflation fears in Japan, the government hopes to fuel consumer spending, a major pillar of the domestic economy. \par \par \uc2  "For Japan, the dollar trading in a range of Y115-Y125 is reasonable," Nakagawa said. "But, given \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  above Y118, I think it would prefer the dollar closer to the top" of that range. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  further to halt any sharp rises in the yen against the dollar but may not take action while the dollar trades above Y120.00, Nakagawa said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0540 GMT, the dollar was trading at Y120.35, up from Y120.24 in New York late Monday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. government also welcomes the Japanese government's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the stubbornly strong yen because the U.S. Treasury Department's strong-dollar policy matches Japan's weak-yen policy, Nakagawa said. \par \par \uc2  "The fact that the U.S. has been silent about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  means the U.S. also desires this," Nakagawa said. \par \par \uc2  Even with the dollar currently trading around Y120, the U.S. administration is comfortable with this level, he added. \par \par \uc2  Underscoring the U.S. administration's acceptance of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said early Tuesday "America praised Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy." \par \par \uc2  However, the U.S. and the euro-zone governments are not expected to join Tokyo in yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in light of those governments' concerns about their export-related industries. \par \par \uc2  On monetary policy, Nakagawa said the BOJ has exhausted all its regular tools to combat deflation and prop up the ailing economy. \par \par \uc2  Last month, the BOJ decided to increase its target for excess liquidity in the market, largely formalizing the emergency steps it took immediately after the terrorist attacks in the U.S., and cut its official discount rate to allow emergency funding to banks. \par \par \uc2  But despite the BOJ carrying out a slew of monetary easing measures to keep the economy from falling into a deflationary spiral, some politicians and economists have called on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to take radical steps such as adopting inflation targets and underwriting Japanese government bonds. \par \par \uc2  Nakagawa rejected the idea of the BOJ introducing inflation targets, saying there are no benefits from such a policy and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't have any tools which it can use to achieve inflation targets. \par \par \uc2  In addition to the BOJ, Nakagawa said, "The government has no more macroeconomic policies which it can use. There are only structural reforms." \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi came to power in April pledging to push forward structural reforms, including financial sector reforms, the write-off of nonperforming loans and fiscal reforms to manage government debt. \par \par \uc2  However, even structural reforms face a rocky path as Japanese banks' profits on their shareholdings are getting hit by the recent weakness of the Japanese stock markets, making it difficult for banks to write off their huge bad loans. \par \par \uc2  Consequently, Japanese banks won't be able to write off their estimated Y30 trillion-Y50 trillion worth of nonperforming assets in the next two to three years unless the government injects public funds into the banks, Nakagawa said. \par \par \uc2  "But the government has said it won't inject public funds into banks, implying bad-loan disposal won't proceed" in a smooth manner over the next three years, he said. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Koizumi, meanwhile, is planning to submit a supplementary budget bill to the Parliament by early November to boost employment, promote structural reform and support the sagging economy. \par \par \uc2  This economic package would likely include direct fiscal spending of Y3 trillion, Nakagawa said. \par \par \uc2  That would push Koizumi over his self-imposed ceiling of Y30 trillion in new government bonds this fiscal year. \par \par \uc2  While such issuance wouldn't have a major adverse effect on Japanese government bonds, Nakagawa said, the JGB market remains sensitive to any uncertainties over supply and demand. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par ron.harui@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011002dxa20006q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
