\par\par \b \uc2  Japan seen carrying on war against yen gains. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Charlotte Cooper \par \uc2 768 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Oktober 2001\par 16:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Oct 2 (Reuters) - Japan won its fight to sap yen strength by September's fiscal half-year end, analysts say, but the aggression it used has convinced markets it is unlikely to stop just because that deadline has passed. \par \par \uc2  September's plane attacks on the United States pitched the dollar into a headlong descent against the yen, prompting the Bank of Japan into a forceful two-week campaign of yen selling. \par \par \uc2  Bankers estimate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  threw a $20-25 billion worth of yen at the market, weakening its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by three percent to 119.50 by New York's close of business on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "For now possibly the Japanese are relatively happy and maybe might leave it for a couple of days but if the dollar does go back down then I imagine once again we will see some action from them," said Derek Halpenny, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi in London. \par \par \uc2  "The market now is coming around to the realisation that what the Bank of Japan has done is far more aggressive than it has been for a long time," he said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said September's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared aimed to smooth and even avert panicked repatriation of funds and export-damaging yen strength. With the economy already fragile, a rising yen would add to its deflationary momentum and hurt exporters' ability to compete abroad. \par \par \uc2  With Friday the last working day of the half year, analysts said there was concern ahead of that deadline that a seasonal flow of funds back into yen to dress up accounts would put upward pressure on the yen. \par \par \uc2  Halpenny said Japan chiefly wanted to avoid a rapid collapse of dollar/yen stemming from potential investor risk aversion in the wake of the U.S. attacks. \par \par \uc2  "They were sending a signal that they didn't want to see significant yen buying by Japanese investors who were repatriating due to renewed risks," he said. \par \par \uc2  IMMEDIATE PRESSURE ABATED \par \par \uc2  Since Friday, the dollar has clawed its way back above 120 yen following a grim Japanese business confidence report and better than expected U.S. manufacturing data, and the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not been seen to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Analysts say that data has for now helped do the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's job for it, bringing Japan's poor fundamentals back to the fore, and as the half-year deadline has passed the immediate pressure is off. \par \par \uc2  "For the time being the yen is going to head south. There seems to have been a change in the market's perception of the yen's performance and this greater desire to go short the yen has some momentum left in it," said Russell Jones, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Lehman Brothers. \par \par \uc2  Market data from last Friday show speculators running net long yen positions, meaning there is scope for more yen sales. Analysts say Japan will be happier with the dollar at 120-125. \par \par \uc2  But should the BOJ return, its impact will depend whether it goes for unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , leaving yen it sells in the market, or sterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , draining those funds out. \par \par \uc2  With markets calmer than they were two weeks ago, there is less need for so much liquidity and future \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be sterilised, which would have a smaller exchange rate impact. \par \par \uc2  CAPITAL FLOWS WILL BE KEY \par \par \uc2  But key to whether the yen's direction going forward will be the balance of current account versus capital account flows. \par \par \uc2  If international events, like some form of U.S. retaliation for the September 11 attacks, led to renewed risk aversion this would encourage Japanese investors to keep funds at home, resulting in less capital outflow which would be yen supportive. \par \par \uc2  In addition, a worsening situation at home, with a weak Japanese stock market and lack of progress on structural reforms within the Japanese economy, was likely to see nervous investors sticking to the relative safety of domestic bonds. \par \par \uc2  "Without the outflow on the capital account, that's not offsetting the inflow on the current account which is in surplus," Halpenny said. \par \par \uc2  Steven Saywell, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank, also expected capital flows to dictate yen direction longer term and expected a range of 115-120 going forward. He said 117 was the focus area when the BOJ started its defence on Sept 17. \par \par \uc2  "117 would seem the level they don't want dollar/yen to go beyond but they have stated it's the speed and the sharpness of the decline which has been of concern for them, rather than the absolute levels," he said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dxa20799o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
