\par\par \b \uc2  GLOBAL MARKETS-Faith in Fed no fillip for Asian stocks. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alan Wheatley, Asian Economics Correspondent \par \uc2 857 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Oktober 2001\par 09:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Oct 2 (Reuters) - Key Asian share markets struggled on Tuesday, dogged by a lower close on Wall Street and what are by now constant background worries about ever-slower growth and retaliation for the deadly strikes on the United States. \par \par \uc2  Even blind trust that Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan later on Tuesday will deliver the ninth cut in U.S. interest rates this year failed to inspire wary investors. \par \par \uc2  In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the dollar drifted down from three-week highs against the yen scaled on a combination of better-than-expected U.S. manufacturing data and speculative buying driven by expectations that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again if necessary to curb the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  Markets in Hong Kong, South Korea, China and Bombay were closed on Tuesday for holidays. \par \par \uc2  In Tokyo, the region's largest bourse, falls on Wall Street provided a cue for investors to lock in profits on Toyota Motor Corp and other blue chips that had climbed during a three-day rally. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei average ended the morning session down 0.46 percent or 45.41 points at 9,926.87. \par \par \uc2  "There's talk of continued pension fund buying at the onset of the second half and the yen's move south should provide support," said Masatoshi Sato, manager of the equity division of Mizuho Investors Securities. \par \par \uc2  "But considering fundamental economic weakness and a shaky performance for U.S. stocks, investors were not ready to push the Nikkei over 10,000," Sato said. \par \par \uc2  In New York, the Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 10.73 points, or 0.12 percent, to 8,836.83, and the Nasdaq composite dropped 18.38 points, or 1.23 percent, to 1,480.42. \par \par \uc2  Shares in Taiwan, which often track the Nasdaq closely, fell 3.91 percent on Tuesday morning, extending a 13 percent fall since the September 11 attacks on New York and Washington, which left more than 5,700 people dead or missing. \par \par \uc2  "Nasdaq's fall and worries over U.S. retaliation against terror attacks triggered selling," said Fubon Securities research manager Norman Lee. \par \par \uc2  WAITING FOR THE FED \par \par \uc2  U.S. stocks retreated despite a conviction that the Federal Reserve will cut interest rates yet again to shore up an economy that many on Wall Street believe is in recession. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters poll on Friday showed that 21 of the 25 primary dealers in bonds expected the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut its key fed funds rate by half a percentage point. \par \par \uc2  Sullen sentiment persisted despite a positive report on U.S. industry: the National Association of Purchasing Management (NAPM) reported that its index of manufacturing activity slipped last month but not nearly as much as investors had feared. \par \par \uc2  Michael Moran, chief economist at Daiwa Securities America Inc, said there was a feeling that the NAPM report had not fully reflected the devastating shock to output and confidence inflicted by last month's suicide airliner attacks. \par \par \uc2  "Many traders and investors felt that (the NAPM index) did not give us a clear read on where the economy stands now," Moran said. \par \par \uc2  Pessimism about global economic prospects certainly weighed on the oil markets, which were also gripped by doubts about the OPEC oil cartel's ability to prop up prices in the face of falling demand. \par \par \uc2  NYMEX crude oil futures extended their losses in electronic trading, with November crude quoted at $23.00 a barrel as of 0223 GMT, down 28 cents from the settlement of $23.28 in New York, where it fell 15 cents. \par \par \uc2  During regular trading in New York, November crude dived as low as $22.40, down $1.03. \par \par \uc2  "After focusing last week on the OPEC meeting, people have come back to worries over the economy," said Chris Schacte of GSC Energy in Atlanta. \par \par \uc2  ECONOMIC PESSIMISM \par \par \uc2  Economic weakness was also the fuel for a rise on Monday in U.S. Treasury bonds, which were additionally buoyed by confidence that the Fed will lower borrowing costs. \par \par \uc2  "Monetary policy and the reality of a recession - those are the invigorating factors for the bond market right now," said Anthony Karydakis, senior financial economist at Banc One Capital Markets. \par \par \uc2  Prices on 30-year U.S. Treasuries, which surged last week on expectations that global economic weakness would depress future inflationary pressures, were up 22/32 to 100, yielding 5.37 percent. \par \par \uc2  Anticipation of another U.S. rate cut also underpinned gold which firmed to US$292.00/2.50 an ounce in early Asian trading from $291.45 at New York's close. \par \par \uc2  Among other Asian share markets, Singapore's Straits Times Index rose 0.30 percent to 1,337.19 but Kuala Lumpur fell 0.20 percent to 612.74. \par \par \uc2  The dollar hovered around 120.20 yen slightly below a peak of 120.45 scaled overnight that was the highest level since the September 11 attacks. \par \par \uc2  The single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was firm, trading around 91.60 cents after dealers in London and New York actively unwound short positions against the yen, Swiss franc and the British pound. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dxa203ny1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
