\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar keeps firm tone vs yen, U.S. data help. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chisa Fujioka \par \uc2 871 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Oktober 2001\par 08:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Oct 2 (Reuters) - The dollar was well supported on Tuesday, helped by slightly better-than-expected news on the U.S. economy and verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese officials. \par \par \uc2  But, though buoyed by Japanese pension funds and foreign investors, the dollar struggled to revisit offshore highs as it continued to face selling interest from domestic exporters. \par \par \uc2  Uncertainty over developments in Afghanistan also dampened gains and gave broad support to the euro. \par \par \uc2  By 0542 GMT, the dollar was at 120.30/35 yen from Monday's late 120.16 yen, but shy of an offshore peak at 120.45. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the passing of September bookclosings for Japan's fiscal half year took some pressure off the dollar as the flow of funds back to Japan from foreign markets eased. \par \par \uc2  Dismal news on the Japanese economy has also helped the greenback, though dealers noted the U.S. economy was in for a tough time as well. \par \par \uc2  "There's some focus back to bad news on the Japanese economy, but there's still plenty of scope for bad numbers coming out on the U.S. economy in the short term," said a Japanese bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Japan's tankan survey of business sentiment on Monday showed corporate confidence slumped in September for a third straight quarter, cementing views the economy was headed for recession. \par \par \uc2  Worries that the yen's recent strength would add to the pain had prompted the Japanese authorities to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem its rise following the September 11 attacks on America. \par \par \uc2  The possibility of further action remained even as the dollar held above 120 yen, as Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa waded into the market with warnings in morning trade. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ready to conduct further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if the dollar fell again. \par \par \uc2  FOMC EYED \par \par \uc2  The dollar was supported by U.S. data overnight. \par \par \uc2  After weeks of unrelenting gloom over the U.S. economy, Monday's National Association of Purchasing Management's (NAPM) monthly index showed slightly better-than-expected results. \par \par \uc2  The NAPM said its monthly index slipped to 47.0 in September after a jump to a nine-month peak of 47.9 in August, beating the market's consensus estimate of 45.5. \par \par \uc2  But as the figure did not entirely factor in the effects of the attacks on New York and Washington, dealers hesitated to buy dollars aggressively. \par \par \uc2  "There's still little optimism about the U.S. economy," said Masanori Hirabayashi, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Shinsei Bank. \par \par \uc2  "It will take a while until we see a recovery in the dollar up to the pre-attack levels of 122 yen," he said. \par \par \uc2  Participants were also waiting for the U.S. Federal Reserve Open Market Committee (FOMC) meeting later in the day, widely expected to deliver a 50-basis-point cut in the Federal funds rate in response to concerns about U.S. economic growth. \par \par \uc2  The dollar is also expected to trade nervously ahead of Friday's employment figures. \par \par \uc2  Economists polled by Reuters estimated that U.S. payrolls shed 109,000 jobs in September after shrinking by 113,000 jobs in August. They put the unemployment rate steady at 4.9 percent. \par \par \uc2  EURO FIRMER VS YEN \par \par \uc2  The yen was on the defensive against the euro, with underlying sentiment for the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  turning bullish after a decisive break through 110 yen . \par \par \uc2  The euro stood at 110.37/45 yen against Monday's 110.22 yen. It climbed as high as 110.47 yen in offshore trade. \par \par \uc2  Against the dollar, the euro held steady around $0.9170/75 against $0.9173. \par \par \uc2  The euro rose on Monday despite a brief setback from reports that German technology group Siemens AG was in talks with Motorola about a joint venture, which prompted speculation of large capital outflows from Europe. \par \par \uc2  But the head of Siemens' mobile phone division later played down the speculation or other forms of cooperation with U.S. rival Motorola. \par \par \uc2  The Wall Street Journal, citing sources familiar with the situation, said on Monday that a deal could create ventures with a total value of between $20 billion and $25 billion. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0616 GMT.  All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 \par \par \uc2  GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                        Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2000 Cls\par                                 01 Oct.\par                        -----------------------------------------\par  Euro/dlr              0.9164   0.9173   -0.10    -3.50   0.9496\par  Dlr/yen               120.46   120.16   +0.25    +5.25   114.45\par  Euro/yen              110.42   110.22   +0.18    +1.57   108.71\par  Stg/dlr               1.4777   1.4791   -0.09    -1.49   1.5000\par  Can/dlr               1.5739   1.5718   +0.13    +5.53   1.4914\par  Aus/dlr               0.4941   0.4952   -0.22   -11.67   0.5594\par  Euro/swiss            1.4824   1.4828   -0.03    -2.61   1.5221\par  Euro/stg              0.6201   0.6196   +0.08    -1.96   0.6325.\par \par \par \par     ..        Debt...      Indicators...\par \f28 \par \par \b \highl