\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen hit by Tankan and BOJ  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  readiness. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Robin Shepherd \par \uc2 781 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2001\par 12:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Oct 1 (Reuters) - The yen fell around quarter of a percent against the dollar and the euro on Monday with analysts attributing weakness in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to economic fundamentals and the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar was slightly firmer against the euro on merger and acquisition talk although it had pared some of its earlier gains which had taken it to an intra-day high of $0.9065. \par \par \uc2  The closely watched Bank of Japan (BOJ) "tankan" quarterly business survey showed Japanese corporate confidence sagged again in September, reinforcing the view that the economy was already back in recession. \par \par \uc2  The Tankan report came against a background of repeated Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  "Last week's BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was very aggressive. The market now is highly aware of the possibility of BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It would be a high risk strategy to buy yen now," said Audrey Childe-Freeman, economist at CIBC World Markets. \par \par \uc2  She added that the tankan survey had refocused attention on the weakness of the Japanese economy. Japanese officials have explained BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in terms of fears that a strong yen could make the country's economic woes even worse. \par \par \uc2  With both the tankan and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in mind, the yen was trading within striking distance of three week lows against the dollar, standing around 119.77 to the dollar at 1010 GMT, while the euro was quoted at 109.14. \par \par \uc2  More broadly, strategists said markets were relatively quiet ahead of Tuesday's interest rate meeting at the U.S Federal Reserve. \par \par \uc2  FIRMER TONE FOR DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The dollar was slightly stronger against the euro, hovering around $0.9110 on speculation of possible large capital outflows out of the euro zone and a better outlook for the U.S. economy after last week's stronger-than-expected data. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the euro was being dented by reports that German technology group Siemens AG was in talks with Motorola about a joint venture and that Germany's WestLB would bid more than 18 billion pounds for British Telecommunications's fixed line telephone network. \par \par \uc2  Siemens said it had no comment on market speculation while an industry source said the state-owned bank was preparing a fresh bid for the network and that it would be higher than 18 billion pounds. \par \par \uc2  "Euro/dollar is trading weaker today, because of some talk of merger and acquisition activity - Siemens and Motorola, WestLB and BT," said Hans Redeker, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas. \par \par \uc2  "This is quite important from a psychological point of view. M&A activity picking up could result in outflows from the euro zone in to the U.S. and Britain." \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that $0.9060 was key support for the euro and a break below that could see losses accelerate to $0.90. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was holding up well against major European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after better-than-expected U.S. growth and consumer sentiment data late last week led to gains on Wall Street. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. economy grew at a slightly stronger pace than previously thought in the second quarter, with the final revision of gross domestic product showing a rise of 0.3 percent, up from the preliminary 0.2 percent. \par \par \uc2  The University of Michigan consumer sentiment survey for September, which partly reflected the September 11 attacks, fell to 81.8 from an August reading of 91.5, but was above economists' forecast of 79.6. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, Reuters Eurozone Purchasing Managers' Index hit the lowest level in its four-year history in September in a survey that underlined the weakness of the region's economy even before the attacks on U.S. cities. \par \par \uc2  The survey showed that manufacturing in the 12-nation euro zone contracted in September at the sharpest pace since the series began in June 1997 as companies shed jobs, cut production and attracted less new business. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the data, which had no major impact on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, would increase pressure on the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  for further cuts in its key refinancing rate, which it reduced to 3.75 percent after the attacks. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese tankan showed the key diffusion index for big manufacturers at minus 33 against minus 16 in the June survey. \par \par \uc2  It was the third straight quarter that the index had worsened and was also below a median forecast of minus 28 in a recent Reuters poll of 19 economists. \par \par \uc2  The tankan also showed big manufacturers predicting that the dollar rate for the second half of the business year would average 115.89 yen, up from 113.08 yen in the June survey. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011001dxa100q38\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
