\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Dollar Faces Tests Under Shadow Of Conflict \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Hardy \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 975 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2001\par 00:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones Australia and New Zealand Report \par \uc2 DJAN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Economic fundamentals are likely to replace \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the driving factor in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets this week, but U.S. military action abroad would throw any prediction out of kilter. \par \par \uc2  One huge unknown continues to hang over short-term forecasts - the chance of U.S.-led military retaliation in Afghanistan or elsewhere for the terrorist attacks on the World Trade Center and Pentagon. How much force is used and against which targets will determine the extent of the reaction in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, according to analysts. A U.S. strike could either help or hurt the dollar, depending on the outcome and repercussions of the action. \par \par \uc2  Barring a military conflict, a number of key issues and data announcements scheduled this week are expected play a major role in determining the dollar's performance against the euro, and more significantly, the yen. \par \par \uc2  In the past week, the dollar has made strong progress against the yen, aided by frequent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against both the U.S. unit and the euro. \par \par \uc2  However, with the Japanese fiscal half-year ending on Monday, many analysts expect repatriation flows into Japan, to slow and believe this factor could weaken the demand for yen and assist the BOJ in its determined effort to prevent the dollar losing more ground to the yen. \par \par \uc2  In light of the significant real-world events that could impact \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, analysts are cautious about forecasting the dollar's near-term this week. \par \par \uc2  However, most expect the euro to maintain its current uptrend, as investors continue to rebalance their portfolios in favor of the euro zone, and see the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moving above 92 cents. \par \par \uc2  The yen is expected to trade nervously with the constant threat of further BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "drawing a line" under the dollar and potentially putting the Y120-Y122 range in view. \par \par \uc2  Adding to the case for a weaker yen, Monday's BOJ quarterly survey of business sentiment (Tankan), a closely-watched indicator of the economic health of the Japanese economy, is expected to be very negative. \par \par \uc2  "The Tankan isn't going to be good," said Larry Greenberg, international economist at Ried, Thunberg in Westport, Conn., who expects the sentiment index to be much lower, with a reading of -31, after June's -16. \par \par \uc2  While Richard Kasmin, vice president of economic research at HotspotFX in Watchung, N.J., agrees the Tankan results "will certainly be dismal," he doesn't expect Japanese institutions to end the repatriation of funds back to Japan and thinks this ongoing demand will prevent any sharp deterioration in the yen's value. \par \par \uc2  "I'm still not convinced that they can keep dollar/yen from slipping," he said. \par \par \uc2  Although he is dismayed by the weak state of the Japanese economy and comments that, "they don't have the commitment for the kind of reforms I think are needed," Greenberg also thinks that the yen's near-term direction remains unclear. \par \par \uc2  "There does seem to be a lot of underlying demand for yen and I don't take the view that it'll end next week," he said. \par \par \uc2  On the dollar side of the equation also, there are several key announcements scheduled which will undoubtedly impact \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the Federal Open Market Committee meets and there seems to be a broad-based expectation of a further 50 basis-point interest rate cut. \par \par \uc2  "I think there's a good chance they'll do 50 (basis-points), because of the drop in consumer confidence," said Kasmin, who feels that this "would give the dollar upside potential." \par \par \uc2  Less convinced of the likelihood of such a cut, Daniel Scheibler, chief economist at Sarasin Investment Bank in Zurich, suggested that there isn't enough new evidence to justify further cuts at this time and commented, "we don't expect the Fed to move." \par \par \uc2  However, Scheibler is quite optimistic over the outlook for the U.S. economy, particularly following Friday's Chicago purchasing management index, which came in at 46.6 in September from 43.5 in August. \par \par \uc2  "We retain a positive view of the U.S. economy," he said, and "for the moment it looks like the dollar has a chance to survive pretty well versus the euro." \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, Scheibler suggested that the Bank of England could cut its interest rates a further 25 basis points this week, despite inflation data that "were not that good." \par \par \uc2  At 1:00 p.m. EDT (1700 GMT) Friday, the dollar was at Y119.13, up from Y119.07 in London but down from Y119.62 in New York Thursday, the euro was at $0.9100, below London's $0.9150 and down from $0.9165 in New York. Sterling was at $1.4708, down from $1.4741 and was also at CHF1.6200, up from CHF1.6129. \par \par \uc2  While monthly U.S. employment data are always a key factor in economic and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  forecasts, there is a sense that Friday's numbers will not carry as much weight as usual. \par \par \uc2  Calling for a drop in payrolls of about 80,000 and a big drop in the index of hours worked, Kasmin played down the value of the numbers. \par \par \uc2  "People might shun it as it'll be hard to get a reading from it," he said, suggesting that analysts may need to wait for the October data to get a better guide to the condition of the economy. \par \par \uc2  Other analysts are in the same area as Kasmin in forecasting the payrolls numbers. Greenberg expects a drop of 100,000, while Scheibler wouldn't be surprised by a decline in the 50,000 to 100,000 range. \par \par \uc2  -By John Hardy, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2122; john.hardy@dowjones \par \par \uc2  1141 4137 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djan000020010930dx9u00001\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
