\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ  \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\uc2  Again As Economy Worsens, Cos Close Books \b0\par\par\uc2  By Arran Scott \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 642 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2001\par 11:45\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets again Friday in an attempt to drive the yen lower after a slew of data showed the economy is deteriorating rapidly, and as companies closed their books for the first half of the fiscal year. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa confirmed the Bank of Japan sold the yen against the dollar Friday morning, the seventh time Tokyo has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen since the Sept. 11 terrorist attacks in the U.S. He warned that the ministry would take further action against speculative moves. \par \par \uc2  The dollar jumped nearly a yen to around Y119.70 on the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though it later fell below Y119.00 - a sign that Japanese authorities haven't yet reversed market sentiment. Still, the effort likely helped to cushion Japan's beleaguered exporters as they converted their foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings into yen for their book closings Friday. \par \par \uc2  Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  underscored Tokyo's determination to stop a strong yen from hamstringing the economy. Thursday, Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi said the government would do all it can to stabilize \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. The sharp rise in the yen in recent weeks threatens to crimp corporate profits and exacerbate deflation, putting further downward pressure on ailing economy. \par \par \uc2  The action came after data revealed the economy continues to deteriorate at a rapid pace. Spending by wage earners dropped 0.8% in August from a year earlier, the fifth straight fall, the government said. The nationwide core consumer price index fell 0.9%. \par \par \uc2  The jobless rate remained at a record high of 5.0% in August, while new job offers fell sharply from July, underscoring the slackening labor market that could hurt consumer spending in months ahead. \par \par \uc2  Industrial production plunged 11.7% in August from a year earlier - the biggest drop on record. Output rose from the previous month for the first time in half a year, but the 0.8% rise was far weaker than expected. METI forecast output will drop 3.8% in July-September. That would be the third straight quarterly drop, highlighting the sharp slowdown in manufacturing, which has been hit hard by weak exports. \par \par \uc2  "August industrial production was an all-round terrible number," said Richard Jerram, economist at ING Barings. \par \par \uc2  The gloom deepened Friday when electronics giants Sony Corp. and NEC Corp. slashed their profit outlooks because of slowing global economic growth and dimmer prospects in the wake of the terrorist attacks in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  The profit warnings knocked down Tokyo stock prices in the afternoon, though the Nikkei 225 average managed to gain 78.15 points to close at 9774.68. Still, that's now far above its recent 18-year closing low of 9504.41 hit Sep. 17. \par \par \uc2  The bleak economic outlook will likely keep the BOJ under the gun to continue pumping money into the economy to help support the private sector and temper price deflation. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  flooded the banking system with nearly Y13 trillion Friday, partly by leaving funds from Wednesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, up more than Y1 trillion from Thursday and more than double its target before the attacks in the U.S. prompted the bank to juice the markets. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ will release Monday a key business sentiment survey, which will likely show how hard the terrorist attacks have hit corporate Japan. The tankan is expected to show a sharp deterioration in companies' view of business conditions and could herald a further retrenchment in private investment. \par \par \uc2  "The mood was bad enough before the terrorist attacks, but those events have caused corporate sentiment to deteriorate at an even faster pace," said Takehiro Sato, economist at Morgan Stanley. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Arran Scott, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par arran.scott@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010928dx9s000mb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
