\par\par \b \uc2  FXAnalytics Focus: Japan Determined Yen Weakens \b0\par\par\uc2  By David Gilmore \par \uc2 777 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2001\par 23:16\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics \par \par \uc2  With over $20 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  estimated in dollar and euro purchases for yen over seven (non-consecutive) days since the September 11 attack on the U.S., and the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  doing what it insisted it never would, leaving \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unsterilized, one can only conclude that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is more determined than ever to prevent the yen from appreciating. And in the current environment of less liquidity, reduced leverage availability for hedge funds and reduced number of large macro hedge funds, going with the BoJ makes more sense than going against the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  While the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in similar volumes for a similar period of time in the past, under \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  instructions, the MoF has never had the full cooperation of the BoJ to the degree it does today. Leaving \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unsterilized is de facto printing yen. This is radical monetary policy and the amounts of yen entering the banking system is not insignificant. The BoJ reported today JPY2.27 trillion ran through the MoF's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  account in September through Thursday. While it is not clear how much if any of this is being sterilized, it is widely assumed that most is not. In a rare remark on the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Wednesday, BoJ Governor Hayami said that a fall in the dollar was not good. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also shifted market expectations of the intent of monetary authorities from stabilizing the yen when \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  first started near 117 area to weakening the yen when the BoJ bought dlr/yen and euro/yen at successively higher levels. Indeed the current view in the market is that the BoJ and MoF want dlr//yen above 120 and perhaps as high as 125. Few can forget what Economy Trade and Industry Minister Hiranuma said earlier in September...115 is due or die for Japanese exporters. MoF and the BoJ are prepared to print as much yen as it takes to keep the yen above 115 and while it has the momentum to try and push it above 120 (nearly achieved after the Tyo fix Friday). \par \par \uc2  What then if anything is missing? G7 participation. It is of little significance if the BoJ has the ECB and Fed operate on its behalf as it did this week. Tactically, when it first happens, it strengthens the BoJ/MoF's hand. But successive \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  where the ECB and Fed act as agents lose their significance. It is far more important if the U.S. and Euro Zone sold yen using their own foreign reserve accounts, indicating a higher degree of cooperation and a more focused goal. Few, however, see this as a reasonable risk ahead given the U.S. economic slump and the blow to U.S. manufacturing from the Sept. 11 tragedy. If NAPM, NAM, AFL-CIO and U.S. farmers were protesting the strong dollar policy in August, they will be especially vocal if the Bush administration agrees to buy dollars for yen. U.S. Treasury is not in a position to begin buying dollars short of a weak dollar creating disorderly U.S. asset markets. No amount of persuasion by the Japanese at the Oct. 06 G7 meeting will convince the U.S. Treasury to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  using the Exchange Stabilization Fund. \par \par \uc2  But all is not for naught for Japan's efforts at getting the yen lower. U.S. Treasury is not opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's unilateral, unsterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ...they may even privately advocate it as a stimulatory measure. Moreover, most of those dollars and euros the BoJ is buying under MoF instructions end up in the US Treasury market and European government bond markets. This will help keep U.S. and Euro Zone market rates down. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  even when unsterilized is not a perfect solution for driving the yen down. But it is a far more effective strategy for countering deflation than other options available. And while G7 may not join in directly, they privately support it as implied in Fed and ECB \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the BoJ. So far it has paid to buy with the BoJ. This trade is still the way to go, especially as fiscal half-year-end repatriation flows wane. \par \par \uc2  -By David Gilmore, \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics; 860-767-9102; fxa@fxa.com \par \par \uc2  (David Gilmore is an economist and partner at \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics, Essex, Conn., which advises institutional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market participants on a real-time basis. Neither the author nor \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics trades in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Opinions expressed are those of the author, and not of Dow Jones Newswires.) \par \par \uc2  1057 4166 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010928dx9s00ct5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
