\par\par \b \uc2  GLOBAL MARKETS-Euro stocks gain, \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  worry curbs yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Cheryl Juckes \par \uc2 1,291 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2001\par 12:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 28 (Reuters) - European shares headed higher on Friday for a fifth straight session, helped by gains in technology and energy stocks, while the yen hovered just above two week lows on fears of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Sentiment has calmed in the absence of any military retaliation to the attacks on the U.S. on September 11. \par \par \uc2  "The recovery we have seen in the last few days will go on," said Achim Matzke, an analyst at Commerzbank in Frankfurt, but he added that that volatility would remain high. \par \par \uc2  "War fear is subsiding. You have support from monetary policy, so it is not a surpise that interest rate-sensitive shares are leading the recovery in Europe," he said. \par \par \uc2  The pan-European FTSE Eurotop 300 index was up 1.95 percent and the blue-chip DJ Euro Stoxx 50 was up 2.38 percent. \par \par \uc2  Both indices have gained about 16 percent from last Friday's three-year lows to show losses of some six percent since the attacks on New York and Washington. \par \par \uc2  Nasdaq 100 futures were up 1.5 percent and S&P 500 futures 0.7 percent higher in early dealings, pointing to a stronger start on Wall Street on top of Thursday's late-session recovery. \par \par \uc2  But those indications could change dramatically, however, if U.S. gross domestic product or consumer confidence figures - due later in the session - contain any surprises. \par \par \uc2  Economists said it was probably too early to assess the full impact of the attacks on the United States but on jobs, the U.S. economic outlook remained shrouded in uncertainty. \par \par \uc2  Attention was turning to the University of Michigan sentiment survey for the whole of September which is due at 1400 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the Michigan data would give a fuller reading of the impact of the attacks on sentiment. \par \par \uc2  A consumer sentiment survey earlier this week showed U.S. confidence suffering its worst fall in 11 years but it captured only part of the shock of this month's attacks on the U.S. \par \par \uc2  BOJ STAMPS ON YEN STRENGTH \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading earlier on Ftriday to curb yen strength for the seventh day in the past two weeks. \par \par \uc2  Similar action via the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the New York Federal Reserve on Thursday pushed the yen to a two-week low on the dollar and a one-month low against the euro. \par \par \uc2  With Japanese authorities selling yen for dollars as the main market influence, the euro was around half a percent down on the dollar and the Swiss franc was also losing ground. \par \par \uc2  The yen was holding around 119.16 per dollar , three quarters of a percent above a two-week low of 120.05 set on Thursday in the wake of determined BOJ sales but nearly three percent off a seven-month peak at 115.76 set last week. \par \par \uc2  Japan's fiscal half-year end book closing takes place on Friday and a perception that Japanese companies have been repatriating funds ahead of this has been buoying the yen. Analysts say the question now is whether the BOJ will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as heavily into the new fiscal half year. \par \par \uc2  Dealers foresaw no imminent end to the BOJ's latest yen-selling campaign, which began shortly before the dollar fell to near seven-month lows last week after suicide aircraft attacks on U.S. cities raised chances the world's biggest economy would tip into recession. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa, confirming Tokyo had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said economic fundamentals warranted a slightly weaker yen and that Tokyo was determined to combat speculative moves to push up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities are seen as keen to stamp out a yen rise which hurts Japanese exporters' earnings, a factor that has weighed on Tokyo's Nikkei share average. \par \par \uc2  ASIAN SHARES RISE \par \par \uc2  Key Asian markets made steady gains on the back of Thursday's late rebound in U.S. blue chip stocks, with Tokyo ending up 0.81 percent boosted by a softer yen. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong shares were up 1.83 percent in afternoon trade and Australia's benchmark closed 1.63 percent higher. But analysts warned gains in many markets would be capped by uncertainty over possible U.S. moves on the weekend in its war on terrorism. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones Industrial Average rose 1.33 percent to 8,681.42 on Thursday, but the tech-driven Nasdaq dipped 0.23 percent, hit by corporate profit warnings and fears of a global recession as the United States braces for military action. \par \par \uc2  Japan was also looking at weak economic data, with industrial output rising just 0.8 percent in August month-on-month, compared with a median forecast of a 2.9 percent rise in a Reuters survey of economists. \par \par \uc2  The weaker-than-expected increase, unemployment stuck at a record high of five percent in August and slower household spending pointed towards Japan tipping into its fourth recession in a decade. \par \par \uc2  SAP LEADS TECH STOCK ADVANCE \par \par \uc2  SAP set the pace for a technology sector bounce tacking on 4.1 percent after its board approved a share buyback of up to 400 million euros. \par \par \uc2  Telecom equipment makers Alcatel and Nokia each added more than two percent, driving a 2.5 percent gain for the DJ Stoxx technology index. \par \par \uc2  Europe's major domestic markets were all trading higher, but the CAC-40 in Paris saw some of its gains evaporate as French electrical equipment maker Schneider and peer Legrand resumed trading sharply lower. \par \par \uc2  Schneider fell 20 percent and Legrand 10 percent after European Commission officials recommended their merger be blocked. Both were halted limit-down earlier. \par \par \uc2  BONDS WANE \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury yields edged up slightly on Friday, moving off lows set in the previous session, as stock futures suggested a positive open on the Wall Street. \par \par \uc2  Traders said two-year yields could revisit record lowse levels if U.S. data later in the day cements expectations that the Federal Reserve will cut rates next week in a bid to restore confidence in the wake of attacks in U.S. cities on September 11. \par \par \uc2  "The problem with Treasuries is that the bond market is obsessed with the equity markets," said Michael O'Sullivan, strategist at Commerzbank. "Equity markets are beginning to look at the prospect of a recovery as recession is pretty much discounted." \par \par \uc2  At 0908 GMT, interest rate-sensitive two-year notes were yielding 2.799 percent, according to data from Tullett & Tokyo Liberty , just above closing level of 2.78 percent in New York on Thursday. Five-year notes were yielding 3.75 percent, still near 38-year lows. \par \par \uc2  Among the economic data scheduled for release on Friday, the U.S. Commerce Department will issue the final revision to second-quarter gross domestic product data at 1230 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Analysts in a Reuters survey forecast on average that GDP crawled forward at a 0.1 percent annual rate in the April to June period, below the previous estimate of 0.2 percent. \par \par \uc2  OIL STEADY \par \par \uc2  Oil prices extended their steady gains with the NYMEX crude oil futures at $22.95 in electronic ACCESS trading, up 21 cents from the New York close. \par \par \uc2  The Organisation of the Petroleum Exporting Countries (OPEC) ratified a pact to hold its oil output steady at 23.2 million barrels per day (bpd) at a meeting in Vienna on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  But Saudi Oil Minister Ali al-Naimi said on Friday he expected OPEC to move swiftly in the next few weeks to prop up prices with output curbs, despite signs of economic recession. (additional reporting by Emily Kaiser, Sabrina Ghani and Charlotte Cooper in London). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011001dx9s00uq9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
