\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen pares gains as  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  renews  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Dhara Ranasinghe \par \uc2 612 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2001\par 14:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 28 (Reuters) - The yen halved earlier gains against the dollar and the euro on Friday after Japan stepped back into the market to stem an export-damaging rise in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The yen had earlier racked up gains of one percent against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as strong demand from Japanese exporters outweighed an earlier attempt by Japanese authorities to weaken the Japanese unit in Asia. \par \par \uc2  But traders betting Japan may be less inclined to meddle with the yen as the fiscal half year draws to a close were caught out as the Bank of Japan waded back into the foreign exchanges in midsession European trade. \par \par \uc2  "Japan has made clear that it wants to see the dollar above 120 yen, and repeated bouts of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  means it is likely to be successful," said Julian Jessop, global market economist at Standard Chartered. \par \par \uc2  "Today's moves have been complicated by the fact it's the last trading day of the fiscal half-year but all fundamentals are pointing to a weaker yen." \par \par \uc2  Repatriation of foreign funds ahead of the end of the fiscal half year have helped boost the yen recently, sending it to seven-month highs versus the dollar last week. \par \par \uc2  Senior Japanese Finance Ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi said Japan had taken "appropriate action" in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. The ministry separately confirmed that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The BOJ, acting on behalf of the finance ministry, has now \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on seven of the past 10 trading days. \par \par \uc2  At 1220 GMT, the yen stood around 119.40 per dollar, holding gains of just a quarter percent on the day. Against the euro, it traded around half a percent higher at 109.15 having pared early gains against both \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  U.S. DATA AWAITED \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the dollar was a firmer around $0.9140 per euro with market attention turning to the University of Michigan consumer sentiment survey due at 1400 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The survey was seen gaining in importance after a report earlier this week showed U.S. consumer confidence suffering its worst fall in 11 years. \par \par \uc2  That survey captured only part of the shock of this month's attacks on the U.S. and economists said the Michigan data would give a better reading of the impact of the attacks on sentiment. \par \par \uc2  Economists polled by Reuters expect, on average, the index to fall to 79.6 in September from 83.6 previously. \par \par \uc2  The final reading of second quarter U.S. growth data was also eyed, with several economists expecting the 0.2 percent quarterly growth figure to be revised lower. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2  SHOWS DETERMINATION \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa confirmed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier during Asian trade and said economic fundamentals warranted a weaker yen. \par \par \uc2  "I think the BOJ will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  because it is worried about the economy," said Steve Barrow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bear Stearns in London. \par \par \uc2  Banking sources in Tokyo said the BOJ had spent $19.40 billion this month up to September 26 to cap the yen's rise against the dollar and euro through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Data released by the BOJ showed the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  paid 2.27 trillion yen to the private sector in September through its special \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  account, which mostly represents \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  The yen was steady on data showing that Japan posted a smaller-than-expected rise in August industrial production, that unemployment held at a record high 5.0 percent and that consumer prices slid for the 23rd straight month. \par \par \uc2  "There have been a large number of exporters buying yen," said another London-based trader. "People were testing the BOJ's resolve." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010928dx9s00opj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
