\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen recovers as corporate demand outweighs BOJ. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Dhara Ranasinghe \par \uc2 626 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2001\par 12:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 28 (Reuters) - The yen rose across the board on Friday, snapping a week-long decline, as buying by Japanese corporates outweighed attempts by Japanese authorities to cap its gains. \par \par \uc2  Japanese banks and companies have been keen to bring home foreign assets ahead of half-year book closing at the end of this month. Dealers said firms were now hedging against future \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure and this was helping to buoy the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Against this backdrop, the Bank of Japan's efforts to sell its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  during Asian trade failed to prevent the yen from rising half a percent against the dollar and as much as one percent against the euro. But wariness of future yen-selling forays persisted. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar/yen has come back from the 120 high but the market is alert to the possibility of the Bank of Japan coming in again," said Neil MacKinnon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Merrill Lynch in London. \par \par \uc2  "Most \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders probably won't want to push dollar/yen down too far." \par \par \uc2  The BOJ stepped into the market for a seventh day in the past two weeks on Friday, selling yen in a bid to prevent its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength from hurting an already frail economy. \par \par \uc2  The yen tumbled to a two-week low against the dollar and one-month trough against the euro on Thursday after Japan enlisted the help of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and New York Federal Reserve on its behalf. \par \par \uc2  But it clawed back to session highs around 118.85 by 1003 GMT to the dollar and held near earlier peaks around 108.75 against the euro. \par \par \uc2  U.S. DATA EYED \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the dollar was a touch firmer around $0.9170 per euro but off the day's highs as market attention turned to the University of Michigan consumer sentiment survey due at 1400 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The survey was seen gaining in importance after a report earlier this week showed U.S. consumer confidence suffering its worst fall in 11 years. \par \par \uc2  That survey captured only part of the shock of this month's attacks on the U.S. and economists said the Michigan data would give a better reading of the impact of the attacks on sentiment. \par \par \uc2  Economists polled by Reuters expect, on average, the index to fall to 79.6 in September from 83.6 previously. The final reading of second quarter U.S. growth data was also eyed, with several economists expecting the 0.2 percent quarterly growth figure to be revised lower. \par \par \uc2  MARKET ON BOJ ALERT \par \par \uc2  Dealers saw no end to the BOJ's latest yen-selling campaign, which began shortly before the dollar fell to multi-month lows following recent suicide attacks on two U.S. cities. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa confirmed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier in the day and said economic fundamentals warranted a weaker yen. \par \par \uc2  "It's been a fairly volatile morning. Dollar/yen has come off a bit after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  and pushed it higher," said Francesca Fornasari, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Lehman in London. \par \par \uc2  "Markets are going to want to see if this increased liquidity the BOJ is leaving in the market is going to continue beyond the fiscal half-year end." \par \par \uc2  The yen showed little reaction to data showing Japan posted a smaller-than-expected rise in August industrial production, unemployment held at a record high 5.0 percent and consumer prices slid for the 23rd straight month. \par \par \uc2  "Yesterday, people tried to push the dollar to 120 yen and once it got there, they have been profit-taking," said one London-based trader. \par \par \uc2  "There is a fear of the BOJ coming into the market and this should support dollar/yen around 118.30." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010928dx9s00kue\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
