\par\par \b \uc2  Asia Markets - Inch higher, suspect worse yet to come. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tamora Vidaillet \par \uc2 1,026 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2001\par 11:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Sept 28 (Reuters) - The value of Asian assets edged up on Friday but only recouped a fraction of their recent losses as investors fear the worst might not yet be over. \par \par \uc2  Gains in most markets were spurred by a mixture of window-dressing ahead of seasonal book closings, tentative gains on Wall Street and limited bargain-hunting. \par \par \uc2  But caution prevailed as investors awaited Washington's next moves in avenging the devastating attacks of September 11, so far centred more on diplomatic overtures than military action. \par \par \uc2  And Philippine stocks headed south to hit a three-year low amid worries that heavyweight conglomerate Benpres Holdings Corp (BPC.might not meet its debt obligations given prospects for a prolonged slowdown, and that other companies might follow suit. \par \par \uc2  Deepening worries over how the attacks would affect the global economy spurred yet more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the export-damaging yen and in turn gave the Tokyo share market a lift. \par \par \uc2  But after several attempts in the past fortnight, the impact on both dollar/yen and yen-sensitive regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was muted. \par \par \uc2  Position squaring also helped dictate market moves as many countries geared up for holidays next week. \par \par \uc2  Public holidays on Monday and Tuesday in Hong Kong and South Korea - where most of the Asian dollar bond trade is done - are expected to drain liquidity from the markets. \par \par \uc2  KOREA DATA POINTS TO EASING \par \par \uc2  With sporadic gains in global stock markets at odds with underlying tensions, analysts looked to statistics for clearer signs of where Asian economies might be headed. \par \par \uc2  South Korea surprised many forecasters with its September inflation and August industrial numbers which came in better than expected. \par \par \uc2  Seasonally adjusted industrial output rose 4.5 percent month-on-month, beating expectations for a 0.9 percent decline as reflected in a recent Reuters poll. \par \par \uc2  The Composite Consumer Price Index CPI), the best gauge of inflation, was unchanged in September compared to a 0.5 percent rise in August. \par \par \uc2  Core inflation, which excludes noncereal and fuel prices, rose 3.6 percent on the year, and was below the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's upper target of four percent for the first time in 2001. \par \par \uc2  That gave more weight to expectations that Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will seize the chance to cut rates again if the Federal Reserve eases U.S. policy next week. \par \par \uc2  "Any fear of inflation, coming primarily from the housing market, is no longer there," Chia Woon Khien, senior economist at ING Barings, told Reuters Television. \par \par \uc2  "So the path for them to go for another rate cut is pretty clear," she said. \par \par \uc2  Despite the possibility of easier credit, the September 11 attacks would have dealt a blow to Korea's economic performance and dashed hopes of a fourth quarter recovery, according to J.P. Morgan Chase. \par \par \uc2  "Indeed there are dire consequences for Korea, given that 20 percent of its exports go to the U.S., where the economy is likely to contract through 02Q1," it said in a research note. \par \par \uc2  Interruptions to export shipments by air, which account for around 25 percent of total exports, would weigh on September export numbers, it said. \par \par \uc2  Thereafter, a likely fall in outward passenger travel and potential liquidity problems among some financial companies could harm the economy, it said. \par \par \uc2  POOR JAPAN DATA \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, statistics from Asia's largest economy deepened the gloom, eclipsing hopes that Japan would be able to help Asia through the economic storm. \par \par \uc2  The chances of Japan averting a fourth recession in a decade appeared to be zero after a slew of data showed the mess the economy was in even before the attacks on the United States. \par \par \uc2  The jobless rate was stuck at all-time highs of five percent, average spending by wage-earning households fell for a fifth month in a row while nationwide consumer prices dropped for a 23rd straight month. \par \par \uc2  A modest 0.8 percent bounce in industrial output - the first in six months - still disappointed as economists had hoped for a gain of nearer 2.9 percent to compensate for steep falls in previous months. \par \par \uc2  So grim were the omens that J.P. Morgan Chase predicted the economy would contract for no less than five consecutive quarters. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  0828 GMT \par \par \uc2   Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen     119.27       118.63         -0.54\par  Sing dlr      1.7663       1.7612         -0.29\par  Taiwan dlr    34.549       34.548         -0.00\par  Korean won   1308.50      1304.25         -0.32\par  Baht           44.43        44.35         -0.18\par  Peso           51.31        51.34         +0.06\par  Rupiah       9700.00      9655.00         -0.46\par  Ringgit       3.7995       3.7995         +0.00\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on year\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2       Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par  Japan yen     119.27       102.21        -14.30\par  Sing dlr      1