\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen claws back ground but wary Japan will act. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Charlotte Cooper \par \uc2 924 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2001\par 10:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 28 (Reuters) - The yen sat nervously above two week lows against the dollar on Friday, with markets on edge in expectation that Japan will not hesitate to stamp out any gains in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Bank of Japan stepped into Asian trading earlier to curb yen strength for the seventh day in the past two weeks, after similar action via the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the New York Federal Reserve on Thursday pushed its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a two-week low on the dollar and a one-month low against the euro. \par \par \uc2  With Japanese authorities selling yen for dollars as the main market influence, the euro was around half a percent down on the dollar and the Swiss franc was also losing ground. \par \par \uc2  "It's been a fairly volatile morning. Dollar/yen has come off a bit after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  and pushed it higher," said Francesca Fornasari, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Lehman in London. \par \par \uc2  "Markets are going to want to see if this increased liquidity the BOJ is leaving in the market is going to continue beyond the fiscal half-year end," she said. \par \par \uc2  Japan's fiscal half-year end book closing takes place on Friday and a perception that Japanese companies have been repatriating funds ahead of this has been buoying the yen. Analysts say the question now is whether the BOJ will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as heavily into the new fiscal half year. \par \par \uc2  The yen was holding around 119.16 per dollar by 0807 GMT, three quarters of a percent above a two-week low of 120.05 set on Thursday in the wake of determined BOJ sales but nearly three percent off a seven-month peak at 115.76 set last week. \par \par \uc2  "Yesterday, people tried to push the dollar to 120 yen and once it got there, they have been profit-taking," said one London-based trader. \par \par \uc2  "There is a fear of the BOJ coming into the market and this should support dollar/yen around 118.30." \par \par \uc2  But Tokyo dealers said the dollar faced pressure after \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as speculators, who had predicted BOJ action, sold on rallies. Exporters were also keen to sell on dollar upticks. \par \par \uc2  Against the euro, the yen recouped three quarters of a percent from an intra-day low of 109.85 per euro to stand at 108.95. \par \par \uc2  As the main beneficiary of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar was half a percent stronger on the day against the euro at $0.9143 and 0.1 percent firmer against the Swiss franc at 1.6142 francs in its strongest performance since September 14. \par \par \uc2  BOJ HAND \par \par \uc2  Dealers saw no end to the BOJ's latest yen-selling campaign, which began shortly before the dollar fell to near seven-month lows last week after suicide aircraft attacks on U.S. cities raised chances the world's biggest economy would tip into recession. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa, confirming Tokyo had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said economic fundamentals warranted a slightly weaker yen and that Tokyo was determined to combat speculative moves to push up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities are seen as keen to stamp out a yen rise which hurts Japanese exporters' earnings, a factor that has weighed on Tokyo's Nikkei share average. \par \par \uc2  Japan has also been suspected of using a weaker yen as a means to ward off deflationary pressures on the economy, though analysts said the theory looked ill-placed given near-zero interest rates and the flood of liquidity already in the market. \par \par \uc2  And while some saw the BOJ easing its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign after the fiscal half-year book-closing on Friday, most said that was unlikely. \par \par \uc2  Although a weaker yen helped companies window-dress books, a mere one-time fix held little meaning in a market working under mark-to-market principles. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar-selling pressure should continue as the flow out of U.S. assets doesn't seem to be easing yet," said Koji Fukaya, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  "It's unlikely that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will stop after the end of the fiscal half-year, when the dollar looks set to start falling again," he said. \par \par \uc2  JAPAN DATA \par \par \uc2  The market reacted little to a dour set of economic data as Japan posted a worse-than-expected rise in August industrial production, unemployment held at a record high 5.0 percent and consumer prices slid for the 23rd straight month. \par \par \uc2  The market instead chose to focus on U.S. data overnight which showed the number of Americans claiming unemployment benefits rose to a nine-year high of 450,000 last week. \par \par \uc2  With economists saying it was probably too early to assess the full impact of the aircraft attacks on the United States on jobs, the U.S. economic outlook remained shrouded in uncertainty. \par \par \uc2  But attention was turning to the University of Michigan sentiment survey for the whole of September which is due at 1400 GMT. A consumer sentiment survey earlier this week showed U.S. confidence suffering its worst fall in 11 years. That survey captured only part of the shock of this month's attacks on the U.S. \par \par \uc2  Economists said however Michigan data would give a better reading of the impact of the attacks on sentiment. \par \par \uc2  "Our U.S. economics team anticipates a decline to 70.0 in the headline September index from 83.6. Such a move would certainly add to bearish near-term dollar sentiment," said Steven Saywell, Citibank senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist in a research note. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010928dx9s00hlu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
