\par\par \b \uc2  GLOBAL MARKETS-Euro stocks gain, \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  curbs yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Cheryl Juckes \par \uc2 1,211 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2001\par 15:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 28 (Reuters) - European shares headed higher on Friday for a fifth straight session led by technology and auto stocks, while on the foreign exchanges the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  yet again to check yen gains. \par \par \uc2  U.S. shares traded in Europe showed a modest rise across a broad front anticipating further momentum gains at the Wall Street open and as sentiment calmed in the absence of any military retaliation for attacks on the United States. \par \par \uc2  "The recovery we have seen in the last few days will go on," said Achim Matzke, an analyst at Commerzbank in Frankfurt, but he added that volatility would remain high. \par \par \uc2  "War fear is subsiding. You have support from monetary policy, so it is not a surprise that interest rate-sensitive shares are leading the recovery in Europe," he said. \par \par \uc2  The pan-European FTSE Eurotop 300 index was up 1.9 percent and the blue-chip DJ Euro Stoxx 50 was 2.1 percent higher. \par \par \uc2  Both indices have gained about 16 percent from last Friday's three-year lows to trim back losses since the attacks on New York and Washington to some six percent. \par \par \uc2  There was more encouraging news on the U.S. economy when the figure for second quarter gross domestic product was revised to 0.3 percent from 0.2 percent. \par \par \uc2  But GDP still turned in its weakest showing since the first quarter of 1993, when the economy shrank 0.1 percent. \par \par \uc2  Attention was turning to the University of Michigan sentiment survey for the whole of September which is due at 1400 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the Michigan data would give a fuller reading of the impact of the attacks on consumer sentiment although it was probably too early to assess the full impact. \par \par \uc2  A consumer sentiment survey earlier this week which showed U.S. confidence suffering its worst fall in 11 years captured only part of the shock of the September 11 attacks. \par \par \uc2  BOJ STAMPS ON YEN STRENGTH \par \par \uc2  Senior Japanese Finance Ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi said on Friday that Japan had taken "appropriate action" in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and the ministry separately confirmed that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  "We have taken an appropriate action in the European market in line with our usual policy stance," Mizoguchi, head of the ministry's international bureau, told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  The yen stood around 119.40 per dollar, holding gains of just a quarter percent on the day. Against the euro, it traded around half a percent higher at 109.15. \par \par \uc2  This is the seventh day in the past two weeks on which Japan has sold yen to halt the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise, which Tokyo fears could harm the nation's fragile and export-dependent economy. \par \par \uc2  Similar action via the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the New York Federal Reserve on Thursday pushed the yen to a two-week low on the dollar and a one-month low against the euro. \par \par \uc2  The yen had earlier racked up gains of one percent against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as strong demand from Japanese exporters outweighed an earlier attempt by Japanese authorities to weaken the yen in Asia. \par \par \uc2  But traders betting Japan may be less inclined to meddle with the yen as the fiscal half year draws to a close at the end of this month were caught out as the Bank of Japan waded back into the foreign exchanges in midsession European trade. \par \par \uc2  "Japan has made clear that it wants to see the dollar above 120 yen, and repeated bouts of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  means it is likely to be successful," said Julian Jessop, global market economist at Standard Chartered. \par \par \uc2  "Today's moves have been complicated by the fact it's the last trading day of the fiscal half-year but all fundamentals are pointing to a weaker yen." \par \par \uc2  Repatriation of foreign funds ahead of the end of the fiscal half year have helped boost the yen recently, sending it to seven-month highs versus the dollar last week. \par \par \uc2  ASIAN SHARES RISE \par \par \uc2  Key Asian markets made steady gains on the back of Thursday's late rebound in U.S. blue chip stocks, with Tokyo ending up 0.81 percent boosted by a softer yen. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones Industrial Average rose 1.33 percent to 8,681.42 on Thursday, but the tech-driven Nasdaq dipped 0.23 percent, hit by corporate profit warnings and fears of a global recession as the United States braces for military action. \par \par \uc2  Japan was also looking at weak economic data, with industrial output rising just 0.8 percent in August month-on-month, compared with a median forecast of a 2.9 percent rise in a Reuters survey of economists. \par \par \uc2  The weaker-than-expected increase, unemployment stuck at a record high of five percent in August and slower household spending pointed towards Japan tipping into its fourth recession in a decade. \par \par \uc2  CARS AND TECHS LEAD ADVANCE \par \par \uc2  Autos and technology shares powered European stock markets ahead. All the DJ Stoxx sector indices were higher, with autos and techs leading the way, up more than three percent apiece. \par \par \uc2  BMW jumped 4.2 percent as Deutsche Bank raised its rating on the stock to "buy" from "market perform". \par \par \uc2  The sector had been under pressure since the attacks on New York and Washington. \par \par \uc2  DaimlerChrsyler added 3.2 percent while Peugeot gained 5.2 percent. \par \par \uc2  Philips Electronics climbed 6.3 percent, leading technology shares higher after a bruising session on Thursday, despite yet another chip sector profit warning, this time from Japan's NEC. \par \par \uc2  Telecom equipment makers Ericsson and Nokia each gained more than three percent. \par \par \uc2  Telecom Italia tacked on 5.6 percent after acquirer Pirelli announced a restructuring plan for the telecoms group. \par \par \uc2  The plan calls for Telecom holding firm Olivetti to slash its debt via six billion euros in asset sales and a four billion euro capital increase. \par \par \uc2  BONDS WANE \par \par \uc2  Most U.S. Treasuries edged higher for a fourth straight session as investors braced for a series of economic reports that are expected to show the economy is weakening. \par \par \uc2  Widespread expectations of another aggressive interest-rate cut from the Federal Reserve when it meets on Tuesday to help defend the economy from a deep recession helped pin short-and intermediate Treasury yields near record lows. \par \par \uc2  Shortly before the New York open, Treasuries cut losses seen in Asian and London trading following a rise in U.S. stock index futures. Safe-haven Treasuries often trade inversely to riskier stocks but on Friday they mostly held their ground on hopes for lower interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "Gains in equities are weighing a little bit," said Kim Rupert, Treasury market analyst at Standard & Poor's MMS. \par \par \uc2  "But I don't see too much weakness in the front end ahead of the Fed meeting on Tuesday." \par \par \uc2  OIL RISES \par \par \uc2  World oil prices moved tentatively higher, bolstered by news that Saudi Arabia sees OPEC moving quickly to defend oil prices. \par \par \uc2  Brent crude futures were last trading 37 cents higher at $23.16 after dipping to a 23-month low of $20.70 on Wednesday because of a gloomy outlook for oil demand amid growing signs of recession. \par \par \uc2  (additional reporting by Ross Finley in New York, Emily Kaiser, Sabrina Ghani and Charlotte Cooper in London). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010928dx9s00fli\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
