\par\par \b \uc2  Yen buried at lows as BOJ  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  again. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chisa Fujioka \par \uc2 606 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2001\par 05:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 28 (Reuters) - The yen was buried at two-week lows against the dollar at midday on Friday, smothered by another round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Japanese monetary authorities. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest foray into the market followed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight for the sixth time in the past two weeks, a move which Japanese officials said involved enlisting the aid of both the New York Federal Reserve and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The New York Fed had declined to comment. \par \par \uc2  "The market is cautious now that authorities have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  around 119.00," said a Japanese bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "But pressure to the dollar's downside is likely to continue with the U.S. economic outlook not looking good," he said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the dollar was already facing pressure after the morning \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as speculators, who had predicted BOJ action, sold on rallies. \par \par \uc2  By late morning, the dollar hovered at 119.24/29 yen from a late offshore level of 119.66. It jumped more than a half-yen on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from a low of 118.95 after the local commercial fix. \par \par \uc2  The yen also pared losses against the euro, with the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 109.06 yen from 109.81 in late New York, but off a one-month peak at 110.22 yen overnight. \par \par \uc2  Against the dollar, the euro eased to 91.47/52 cents from 91.79. \par \par \uc2  BOJ HAND \par \par \uc2  Dealers saw no end to the BOJ's latest yen-selling campaign, which started as the dollar fell to near seven-month lows last week after suicide aircraft attacks on U.S. cities raised chances the world's biggest economy would tip into recession. \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities are seen keen to stamp out a yen rise which hurts Japanese exporters' earnings, a factor that has weighed on Tokyo's Nikkei share average. \par \par \uc2  Japan has also been suspected of using a weaker yen as a means to ward off deflationary pressures on the economy, though analysts said the theory looked ill-placed given near-zero interest rates and the flood of liquidity already in the market. \par \par \uc2  And while some saw the BOJ easing its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign after the fiscal half-year book-closing on Friday, most said that was unlikely. \par \par \uc2  Although a weaker yen helped companies window-dress books, a mere one-time fix held little meaning in a market working under mark-to-market principles. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar-selling pressure should continue as the flow out of U.S. assets doesn't seem to be easing yet," said Koji Fukaya, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  "It's unlikely that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will stop after the end of the fiscal half-year, when the dollar looks set to start falling again," he said. \par \par \uc2  JAPAN DATA \par \par \uc2  The market reacted little to a set of dour economic data from Japan, with dealers saying the yen already reflected the grim state of the economy. \par \par \uc2  Japan posted on Friday a worse-than-expected rise in August industrial production, but the news hardly caused a ripple in the market. \par \par \uc2  Data also showed Japan's unemployment rate stuck at a record high 5.0 percent in August, while consumer prices slid for the 23rd straight month. \par \par \uc2  The market instead chose to focus on U.S. data overnight which showed the number of Americans claiming unemployment benefits rose to a nine-year high of 450,000 last week. \par \par \uc2  With economists saying it was probably too early to assess the full impact of the aircraft attacks on the United States on jobs, the U.S. economic outlook remained shrouded in uncertainty. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010928dx9s00epc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
